WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.645 --> 00:00:05.228
(mellow music)

00:00:07.118 --> 00:00:08.218
- David look!

00:00:08.218 --> 00:00:10.595
They're wheels!
- Wheels?

00:00:10.595 --> 00:00:13.595
(foreign language)

00:00:22.180 --> 00:00:24.920
- What a wonderful day to
be in the house of the Lord.

00:00:24.920 --> 00:00:28.860
If you're here with us for
the first time, please stand.

00:00:28.860 --> 00:00:30.740
What a beautiful family.

00:00:30.740 --> 00:00:31.763
Glad you're here.

00:00:34.860 --> 00:00:36.780
- How's your daddy like that new farm.

00:00:36.780 --> 00:00:39.382
He growin' things good,
doing things right?

00:00:39.382 --> 00:00:40.215
- Yes.

00:00:41.186 --> 00:00:44.490
- (foreign language)

00:00:44.490 --> 00:00:46.441
- But I don't like grandma.

00:00:46.441 --> 00:00:49.050
- (foreign language)

00:00:49.050 --> 00:00:51.138
- Grandma smells like Korea.

00:00:51.138 --> 00:00:54.514
- (foreign language)

00:00:54.514 --> 00:00:57.210
(snoring)

00:00:57.210 --> 00:00:59.359
(tender music)

00:00:59.359 --> 00:01:02.442
- (foreign language)

00:01:08.113 --> 00:01:10.363
(shouting)

00:01:15.321 --> 00:01:18.404
- (foreign language)

00:01:29.188 --> 00:01:31.360
- We need to find water somewhere.

00:01:31.360 --> 00:01:33.903
You got soil ain't wet,
you're gonna lose your crop.

00:01:39.300 --> 00:01:42.383
- (foreign language)

00:01:48.846 --> 00:01:51.596
(sweeping music)

00:01:56.021 --> 00:01:58.022
- Oh, you pretty K boy.

00:01:58.022 --> 00:01:59.497
Pretty K boy.

00:01:59.497 --> 00:02:02.414
- I'm not pretty, I'm good looking!

00:02:03.401 --> 00:02:05.568
(chuckle)

00:02:08.670 --> 00:02:10.310
- Hello everyone, and welcome to

00:02:10.310 --> 00:02:14.090
the Academy's Visiting Artists
Film Series on "Minari,"

00:02:14.090 --> 00:02:17.610
a conversation with
writer/director Lee Isaac Chung.

00:02:17.610 --> 00:02:19.930
My name is Janet Yang, and I'm a governor

00:02:19.930 --> 00:02:22.450
at the Academy of Motion
Pictures Arts and Sciences

00:02:22.450 --> 00:02:24.590
in the Producer's Branch.

00:02:24.590 --> 00:02:28.360
I am also an officer chairing
its membership committee.

00:02:28.360 --> 00:02:31.510
And now I'd like to introduce
to you Lee Isaac Chung.

00:02:31.510 --> 00:02:33.200
Hello Lee Isaac.
- Oh, hello, Janet.

00:02:33.200 --> 00:02:34.750
- The last time I saw you

00:02:34.750 --> 00:02:39.430
was at the nominees dinner
for producers and directors,

00:02:39.430 --> 00:02:42.460
and we were very wise to
bring back those like you

00:02:42.460 --> 00:02:45.340
who were nominated previously last year

00:02:45.340 --> 00:02:48.043
but didn't get a chance to
celebrate you in person.

00:02:48.990 --> 00:02:53.990
So, I wanna take you back to that time,

00:02:54.140 --> 00:02:56.820
because one thing that's
gone viral, I think,

00:02:56.820 --> 00:02:57.653
on the internet,

00:02:57.653 --> 00:02:59.830
is when you were informed
of your nomination,

00:02:59.830 --> 00:03:01.570
and your adorable daughter,

00:03:01.570 --> 00:03:04.480
who was fully prepared for the moment,

00:03:04.480 --> 00:03:05.480
moreso perhaps than you,

00:03:05.480 --> 00:03:06.930
what did it feel like at the moment

00:03:06.930 --> 00:03:08.503
to be honored in that way?

00:03:11.330 --> 00:03:14.170
- Yeah, that was quite a
(chuckle) strange time.

00:03:14.170 --> 00:03:15.680
That was a year ago.

00:03:15.680 --> 00:03:19.590
And we were juggling the
effects of the pandemic

00:03:19.590 --> 00:03:24.300
along with this incredible
awards run for the film.

00:03:24.300 --> 00:03:28.870
So it was this weird
thing to be going through.

00:03:28.870 --> 00:03:31.730
I think that thing with my
daughter was the Golden Globes.

00:03:31.730 --> 00:03:32.907
That's when that happened.
- Oh it was the Golden Globes,

00:03:32.907 --> 00:03:33.950
I see.
- Yeah,

00:03:33.950 --> 00:03:37.170
when the announcement for
the Oscars happened, I was,

00:03:37.170 --> 00:03:38.340
honestly, I slept.

00:03:38.340 --> 00:03:40.740
'Cause I thought, I don't wanna, like,

00:03:40.740 --> 00:03:41.900
assume I'm gonna get nominated.

00:03:41.900 --> 00:03:44.050
I'm not gonna watch the nominations.

00:03:44.050 --> 00:03:46.890
And then I woke up and
found that I had, like,

00:03:46.890 --> 00:03:48.320
hundreds and hundreds of texts,

00:03:48.320 --> 00:03:51.180
and that's when I realized
something happened.

00:03:51.180 --> 00:03:52.900
So yeah, it's just weird.

00:03:52.900 --> 00:03:54.844
I never get hundreds of texts.

00:03:54.844 --> 00:03:57.940
- (laugh) Well, you're
probably getting more texts now

00:03:57.940 --> 00:03:59.040
than you did before it.

00:03:59.040 --> 00:04:01.590
You also had been so celebrated

00:04:01.590 --> 00:04:03.780
at the Sundance Film
Festival earlier in the year.

00:04:03.780 --> 00:04:07.620
So, you probably got used to
getting a lot of attention.

00:04:07.620 --> 00:04:10.080
And you hadn't made a film in a while.

00:04:10.080 --> 00:04:13.093
Did you have a feeling about that,

00:04:13.093 --> 00:04:17.690
and how far the film would
be embraced so widely?

00:04:17.690 --> 00:04:19.490
- No, I didn't.

00:04:19.490 --> 00:04:21.965
No, I'm not getting used to the attention.

00:04:21.965 --> 00:04:23.300
(Janet chuckles)

00:04:23.300 --> 00:04:27.550
Yeah, it's weird, 'cause
this one was a film

00:04:27.550 --> 00:04:31.691
in which I probably was
thinking the least about, like,

00:04:31.691 --> 00:04:34.003
would this get an award
or would this do well?

00:04:37.260 --> 00:04:38.093
Maybe that's not true.

00:04:38.093 --> 00:04:40.050
My first film, I was kinda like that.

00:04:40.050 --> 00:04:41.730
And it was such a pure process.

00:04:41.730 --> 00:04:44.270
And then I made two films after that.

00:04:44.270 --> 00:04:49.210
And that kind of idea
of getting some notice

00:04:49.210 --> 00:04:51.533
was heavily in my mind.

00:04:51.533 --> 00:04:55.080
And those films didn't
turn out to do that for me.

00:04:55.080 --> 00:04:58.820
So, I had a difficult go
at it for a few years.

00:04:58.820 --> 00:05:03.070
And this film ended up being
almost like a swan song.

00:05:03.070 --> 00:05:05.350
Like, I wondered if I
could even get it made.

00:05:05.350 --> 00:05:08.320
It was maybe the last
thing I would ever write,

00:05:08.320 --> 00:05:09.370
that's what I was thinking.

00:05:09.370 --> 00:05:10.830
I went off to teach.

00:05:10.830 --> 00:05:15.380
I was also Professor
Chung in Korea. (chuckle)

00:05:15.380 --> 00:05:17.670
You know, I just went off and taught,

00:05:17.670 --> 00:05:20.120
and I thought maybe I'll
never make a film again.

00:05:21.050 --> 00:05:23.880
And so when I was given a
chance to make this film,

00:05:23.880 --> 00:05:25.340
my whole idea with it

00:05:25.340 --> 00:05:27.350
was just to be as personal as possible,

00:05:27.350 --> 00:05:30.360
just to relish the
experience of making a film.

00:05:30.360 --> 00:05:34.310
And just to go back to the purity

00:05:34.310 --> 00:05:36.570
of why it is we tell stories.

00:05:36.570 --> 00:05:40.560
Like to love people, to
get into their stories,

00:05:40.560 --> 00:05:42.450
to inspire audiences.

00:05:42.450 --> 00:05:44.670
You know, all these different
things that I just think,

00:05:44.670 --> 00:05:48.333
cinema, when it's clicking
is so wonderful and amazing.

00:05:49.240 --> 00:05:52.960
So, all the other stuff
that's happened since then,

00:05:52.960 --> 00:05:54.863
honestly I wonder if it's real.

00:05:56.159 --> 00:05:58.271
It's kind of unbelievable.

00:05:58.271 --> 00:06:01.060
But I'm just so touched
that people, I think,

00:06:01.060 --> 00:06:04.550
are responding to the joy and purity

00:06:04.550 --> 00:06:06.120
with which we made the film.

00:06:06.120 --> 00:06:08.730
'Cause I think we were all
trying to be very vulnerable

00:06:08.730 --> 00:06:11.343
with each other and with the material.

00:06:12.185 --> 00:06:14.300
So just to see people responding to it

00:06:14.300 --> 00:06:16.883
has been really, really beautiful.

00:06:17.960 --> 00:06:20.670
- Do you think it's a sign
of the changing times?

00:06:20.670 --> 00:06:24.793
Because I am very encouraged,
as I think you are,

00:06:25.880 --> 00:06:27.590
about the attention that Asians

00:06:27.590 --> 00:06:30.410
and Asian Americans are getting on screen.

00:06:30.410 --> 00:06:31.410
And I like to think

00:06:31.410 --> 00:06:33.170
this is something that is not going away,

00:06:33.170 --> 00:06:35.180
that it's just the tip of the iceberg,

00:06:35.180 --> 00:06:36.500
and there's more and more

00:06:36.500 --> 00:06:39.450
authentic voices that can be embraced.

00:06:39.450 --> 00:06:41.250
Your film was a game changer.

00:06:41.250 --> 00:06:43.650
Others in recent years have been as well.

00:06:43.650 --> 00:06:46.450
I think it's no surprise, you
know, "Crazy Rich Asians,"

00:06:46.450 --> 00:06:49.220
or "Parasite," or "Drive My Car."

00:06:49.220 --> 00:06:52.130
But yours, it was such an intimate film.

00:06:52.130 --> 00:06:56.890
And precisely because it seemed
so deeply felt and personal,

00:06:56.890 --> 00:07:00.730
I think people widely loved it,

00:07:00.730 --> 00:07:04.180
whether or not you could relate
to this particular family.

00:07:04.180 --> 00:07:09.180
Do you see that as being a
sign of what is happening now?

00:07:11.700 --> 00:07:14.050
- I mean, I appreciate that.

00:07:14.050 --> 00:07:17.200
I do see a lot of reasons to hope.

00:07:17.200 --> 00:07:20.770
I think "Joy Luck Club" did
very well when it came out.

00:07:20.770 --> 00:07:23.810
I kinda feel like it's an issue in which

00:07:23.810 --> 00:07:27.450
it's not just that audiences
are embracing films like this,

00:07:27.450 --> 00:07:29.280
but it's that I think

00:07:29.280 --> 00:07:32.120
we're being given more opportunities now.

00:07:32.120 --> 00:07:36.440
And I think when filmmakers
of various backgrounds

00:07:36.440 --> 00:07:38.550
are able to have those opportunities,

00:07:38.550 --> 00:07:39.800
what we see is that

00:07:41.140 --> 00:07:43.750
audiences actually wanna see those films.

00:07:43.750 --> 00:07:45.580
And I think "Joy Luck Club" proved that.

00:07:45.580 --> 00:07:48.210
And a lot of these films that
are coming out these days

00:07:48.210 --> 00:07:49.440
are kinda proving that as well,

00:07:49.440 --> 00:07:52.120
which has been really wonderful.

00:07:52.120 --> 00:07:53.270
That's one of the great things

00:07:53.270 --> 00:07:55.210
about all the platforms
we have to watch films,

00:07:55.210 --> 00:07:59.060
is that it's truly made
things a lot more diverse.

00:07:59.060 --> 00:08:00.030
- Yeah.

00:08:00.030 --> 00:08:05.030
Well, your film was just a joy to watch.

00:08:06.500 --> 00:08:08.913
The pain, the depth, the heart.

00:08:09.870 --> 00:08:12.140
One of the things that
does make it unusual is

00:08:12.140 --> 00:08:14.560
it doesn't fall into
any tropes whatsoever.

00:08:14.560 --> 00:08:17.830
Because we have, for instance, on TV now,

00:08:17.830 --> 00:08:19.070
there's three different shows

00:08:19.070 --> 00:08:21.250
about Asian Americans who do martial arts.

00:08:21.250 --> 00:08:23.360
And more power to them.

00:08:23.360 --> 00:08:25.150
But yours was something

00:08:25.150 --> 00:08:28.140
that took place outside
of an Asian community,

00:08:28.140 --> 00:08:30.050
in the heart of the Midwest.

00:08:30.050 --> 00:08:34.440
And do you think that added
to the relatability of it?

00:08:34.440 --> 00:08:37.923
It is obviously related
to a personal story.

00:08:42.320 --> 00:08:45.420
You probably have had
some time to think about

00:08:45.420 --> 00:08:49.070
why this film has been just so beloved.

00:08:49.070 --> 00:08:51.393
And do you think that's
one of the reasons?

00:08:53.090 --> 00:08:55.320
- Well, you know, I
didn't go into this one

00:08:55.320 --> 00:08:58.163
thinking about whether
it's marketable or not.

00:08:59.390 --> 00:09:02.970
And I wonder if there are some stories

00:09:02.970 --> 00:09:04.320
that get made because

00:09:04.320 --> 00:09:07.510
that kind of gets put in
front of the cart in a way.

00:09:07.510 --> 00:09:09.953
That's the horse that's
put in front of the cart.

00:09:10.830 --> 00:09:13.380
For me, this was just
a very personal story,

00:09:13.380 --> 00:09:15.700
and it comes from the way that I grew up.

00:09:15.700 --> 00:09:20.700
And I wasn't putting the
issues first, in a way.

00:09:22.390 --> 00:09:25.270
I was putting more the emotions

00:09:25.270 --> 00:09:29.260
and the spiritual dynamics
of the story first.

00:09:29.260 --> 00:09:32.293
Like, the idea of love within this family.

00:09:32.293 --> 00:09:34.670
A family really trying
hard to love each other,

00:09:34.670 --> 00:09:36.523
and having a hard time at it.

00:09:37.930 --> 00:09:38.763
And I think

00:09:38.763 --> 00:09:41.790
that's what it seems people
are responding to with it.

00:09:41.790 --> 00:09:44.253
And I recognize that.

00:09:45.430 --> 00:09:47.549
In a lot of films that I watch,

00:09:47.549 --> 00:09:52.549
it's always the human
qualities of the films

00:09:52.680 --> 00:09:53.780
that seem to resonate with me.

00:09:53.780 --> 00:09:56.500
It doesn't matter where
those films are from.

00:09:56.500 --> 00:10:00.020
For instance, I loved
"Worst Person In the World,"

00:10:00.020 --> 00:10:02.800
you know, of this past
year, and "Drive My Car."

00:10:02.800 --> 00:10:06.780
And these are very, you know,
films from other cultures,

00:10:06.780 --> 00:10:08.780
but I still really felt like

00:10:08.780 --> 00:10:12.293
I was connecting very
deeply with the filmmakers.

00:10:13.670 --> 00:10:16.750
So I kinda hope that's
what it was with this one.

00:10:16.750 --> 00:10:21.650
It's really hard to take
this as a case study for me,

00:10:21.650 --> 00:10:23.270
even moving forward,

00:10:23.270 --> 00:10:26.380
to figure out like, how do
I reach an audience again.

00:10:26.380 --> 00:10:29.560
It's just, yeah, such a mystery.

00:10:29.560 --> 00:10:32.950
- I think you do it with your
heart, like you did with this.

00:10:32.950 --> 00:10:36.770
But several of the
characters in your movie

00:10:36.770 --> 00:10:38.190
are, again, surprises.

00:10:38.190 --> 00:10:39.410
I appreciate it

00:10:39.410 --> 00:10:41.930
because I've seen just about
everything there is to see

00:10:41.930 --> 00:10:44.960
about representations of Asians

00:10:44.960 --> 00:10:46.640
and Asian Americans on screen.

00:10:46.640 --> 00:10:49.430
But the grandmother
character, Youn Yuh-jung,

00:10:49.430 --> 00:10:53.340
who gave that fabulous Oscar
speech (chuckle) last year.

00:10:53.340 --> 00:10:56.040
She also is not at all your typical.

00:10:56.040 --> 00:10:57.550
She's feisty.

00:10:57.550 --> 00:11:00.600
She's not this kind of
long suffering grandmother

00:11:00.600 --> 00:11:01.880
that's sacrificing everything.

00:11:01.880 --> 00:11:03.290
She has her own life.

00:11:03.290 --> 00:11:08.290
She has a troubled relationship
initially with her grandson.

00:11:08.850 --> 00:11:11.030
How did you shape this character?

00:11:11.030 --> 00:11:12.883
Is she someone in your life?

00:11:14.190 --> 00:11:15.490
- Yeah, I think (chuckle),

00:11:17.570 --> 00:11:18.610
before you came on, Janet,

00:11:18.610 --> 00:11:21.460
I was mentioning just how
fictional these characters are.

00:11:22.660 --> 00:11:25.870
But in this case,
(Janet chuckles)

00:11:25.870 --> 00:11:27.360
I gotta say, I don't know

00:11:27.360 --> 00:11:29.650
what a quote unquote
typical grandmother is,

00:11:29.650 --> 00:11:31.960
because I was partly raised

00:11:31.960 --> 00:11:33.810
by my grandma who came from Korea,

00:11:33.810 --> 00:11:37.585
and she drove me nuts.

00:11:37.585 --> 00:11:40.323
(laugh) She drove me and my sister nuts.

00:11:41.857 --> 00:11:45.300
She loved to annoy us,

00:11:45.300 --> 00:11:48.543
and do these things that
would just set us off.

00:11:49.910 --> 00:11:52.490
And at the same time, she
was tremendously loving,

00:11:52.490 --> 00:11:55.863
and she was really a best
friend for me growing up.

00:11:57.090 --> 00:12:00.470
And she just wasn't, I dunno,

00:12:00.470 --> 00:12:05.470
she wasn't doing the sorts of things

00:12:05.840 --> 00:12:09.470
that you might expect a
grandmother to be doing.

00:12:09.470 --> 00:12:10.610
She didn't seem, I guess,

00:12:10.610 --> 00:12:13.080
like a lotta the grandmothers
who I met around me

00:12:13.080 --> 00:12:14.843
in Lincoln, Arkansas.

00:12:15.830 --> 00:12:17.860
So it was always hard
to describe to people

00:12:17.860 --> 00:12:19.670
what my grandma's like.

00:12:19.670 --> 00:12:23.530
And sadly, it seemed like
nobody else kind of knew her,

00:12:23.530 --> 00:12:25.790
because she couldn't speak the language.

00:12:25.790 --> 00:12:28.391
She spent most of her time with us.

00:12:28.391 --> 00:12:30.440
So a lotta people just didn't see her.

00:12:30.440 --> 00:12:33.240
And that was one of the
elements of this film

00:12:33.240 --> 00:12:34.310
that I wanted to do,

00:12:34.310 --> 00:12:39.030
was to kind of reveal this
person who I felt was invisible,

00:12:39.030 --> 00:12:41.163
try to make someone like that visible.

00:12:42.550 --> 00:12:45.530
- And what about your
other casting choices?

00:12:45.530 --> 00:12:48.890
It just was like a
perfectly assembled family.

00:12:48.890 --> 00:12:50.000
What were the challenges,

00:12:50.000 --> 00:12:53.180
and who did you have in mind early on,

00:12:53.180 --> 00:12:55.593
or who came late to the team?

00:12:56.540 --> 00:12:59.660
- Yeah, so when I'm writing,

00:12:59.660 --> 00:13:02.660
usually I don't have anybody in mind.

00:13:02.660 --> 00:13:05.050
I had Will Patton in mind for Paul.

00:13:05.050 --> 00:13:07.853
That was the only person
I had in mind as I wrote.

00:13:09.430 --> 00:13:14.040
And I think Steven came on first.

00:13:14.040 --> 00:13:17.920
And that was because we already
kind of knew each other.

00:13:17.920 --> 00:13:19.363
He's married to my cousin,

00:13:20.230 --> 00:13:22.060
my first cousin who grew up in Arkansas.

00:13:22.060 --> 00:13:24.350
So we kinda knew each other.

00:13:24.350 --> 00:13:27.590
But we also started to be
represented by the same agent

00:13:27.590 --> 00:13:29.090
around the same time.

00:13:29.090 --> 00:13:31.550
And she kind of connected us
both knowing this project,

00:13:31.550 --> 00:13:34.300
saying, "Hey, you guys should
work together on this."

00:13:34.300 --> 00:13:37.570
And I was really scared.

00:13:37.570 --> 00:13:40.050
I didn't wanna mess up a
family (chuckle) relationship.

00:13:40.050 --> 00:13:43.010
And I wasn't sure if he'd wanna do this,

00:13:43.010 --> 00:13:44.447
because he'd have to speak with an accent

00:13:44.447 --> 00:13:45.720
the whole time as well.

00:13:45.720 --> 00:13:48.260
And you know how charged that can be,

00:13:48.260 --> 00:13:52.200
that sort of issue, for Asian actors.

00:13:52.200 --> 00:13:56.120
So I was really pleasantly
surprised when we connected

00:13:56.120 --> 00:13:59.343
and he loved the script
and wanted to do it.

00:14:00.640 --> 00:14:03.790
So, he was the first Yi family,

00:14:03.790 --> 00:14:06.440
person I had on my family picture frame

00:14:06.440 --> 00:14:09.790
that I was building out as
I was prepping the film.

00:14:09.790 --> 00:14:14.790
And I was able to fill in Youn
Yuh-jung and Han Ye-ri next.

00:14:14.960 --> 00:14:16.900
And I was living in Korea at the time,

00:14:16.900 --> 00:14:19.630
so I was able to meet them in person.

00:14:19.630 --> 00:14:24.630
And Youn Yuh-jung was
just a dream to work with.

00:14:25.840 --> 00:14:30.178
And I just jumped at the chance,

00:14:30.178 --> 00:14:34.870
that if she could do it,
I would work with her.

00:14:34.870 --> 00:14:37.350
So a mutual friend of
ours put us in touch,

00:14:37.350 --> 00:14:38.970
and we met together.

00:14:38.970 --> 00:14:40.160
I think she was only interested

00:14:40.160 --> 00:14:42.100
in whether or not this story was real.

00:14:42.100 --> 00:14:43.900
That's what she asked.

00:14:43.900 --> 00:14:45.570
Like, is this a real story?

00:14:45.570 --> 00:14:46.810
Yes, I'll do it.

00:14:46.810 --> 00:14:48.360
No, (laugh) that was kind of it.

00:14:48.360 --> 00:14:50.840
- Oh, and you told her
it was a real story.

00:14:50.840 --> 00:14:51.855
- I did.

00:14:51.855 --> 00:14:52.688
I mean,
(Janet chuckles)

00:14:52.688 --> 00:14:53.521
I kinda told her-
- Little white lie.

00:14:53.521 --> 00:14:55.910
- How it was written, yeah.

00:14:55.910 --> 00:14:59.900
And then Han Ye-ri also
grew up on a farm in Korea.

00:14:59.900 --> 00:15:03.650
So there was a similarity there.

00:15:03.650 --> 00:15:06.920
So when we got together,
we didn't talk much.

00:15:06.920 --> 00:15:08.770
Like, we were pretty
quiet with each other.

00:15:08.770 --> 00:15:10.690
But there was just a shared comfort.

00:15:10.690 --> 00:15:13.343
And I kinda felt like we
understood each other.

00:15:14.660 --> 00:15:16.930
So that's how they came about to be,

00:15:16.930 --> 00:15:20.370
like, those three got
involved in the project.

00:15:20.370 --> 00:15:22.360
And then the big challenge,
to answer your question,

00:15:22.360 --> 00:15:23.648
was finding the kids.

00:15:23.648 --> 00:15:25.453
That was really tough.

00:15:26.850 --> 00:15:29.540
We had maybe two months to find them.

00:15:29.540 --> 00:15:34.540
And we knew that they would
be most likely non-actors,

00:15:35.950 --> 00:15:37.800
people who have not acted before.

00:15:37.800 --> 00:15:42.800
And we had this wonderful
casting agent, Julia Kim.

00:15:42.900 --> 00:15:47.900
Just scoured Korean churches and hagwons,

00:15:48.517 --> 00:15:52.360
and different places where
Korean kids are congregating.

00:15:52.360 --> 00:15:54.560
And we were just looking for a Korean kid

00:15:54.560 --> 00:15:58.780
who'd be able to speak in
Korean pretty fluently,

00:15:58.780 --> 00:16:03.650
and also can act, (chuckle)
and all these other things.

00:16:03.650 --> 00:16:04.860
So it was just a miracle

00:16:04.860 --> 00:16:09.258
that we found both Alan
Kim and Noel Kate Cho.

00:16:09.258 --> 00:16:10.730
They were just incredible.

00:16:10.730 --> 00:16:15.070
We had them get together
on Zoom, kinda like this,

00:16:15.070 --> 00:16:16.160
and just talk to each other

00:16:16.160 --> 00:16:17.440
as though they're brother and sister.

00:16:17.440 --> 00:16:19.220
And we saw them clicking.

00:16:19.220 --> 00:16:23.130
And Noel was, like, asking him
if he did all his homework.

00:16:23.130 --> 00:16:27.020
And, you know, she was
saying all these things that,

00:16:27.020 --> 00:16:30.370
I was just watching, like, a
family conversation happening.

00:16:30.370 --> 00:16:31.440
It was really magical.

00:16:31.440 --> 00:16:36.020
And then Steven came
in for Alan's audition

00:16:36.020 --> 00:16:38.450
and actually did some improv with Alan.

00:16:38.450 --> 00:16:41.400
And Alan sat on his lap.

00:16:41.400 --> 00:16:45.320
And I just saw a dad and his son.

00:16:45.320 --> 00:16:47.290
And I thought, wow,
this is gonna be great.

00:16:47.290 --> 00:16:49.957
So that's how we ended up going with them.

00:16:49.957 --> 00:16:52.815
- And that Alan Kim, he's a superstar.

00:16:52.815 --> 00:16:54.255
(laugh) He has a huge
following on social media.

00:16:54.255 --> 00:16:55.840
- [Lee Isaac] (laugh) Yeah, I know.

00:16:55.840 --> 00:16:57.910
- You said about two or
three things just now

00:16:57.910 --> 00:16:59.870
that I wanted to follow up on.

00:16:59.870 --> 00:17:01.230
One is the importance, I think,

00:17:01.230 --> 00:17:03.370
of having a casting director,

00:17:03.370 --> 00:17:06.280
having an entire crew, for that matter,

00:17:06.280 --> 00:17:08.730
that really understands and
can relate to the story.

00:17:08.730 --> 00:17:12.750
I mean, I can't imagine somebody
helping you with casting

00:17:12.750 --> 00:17:15.710
and not being able to
get into Korean churches,

00:17:15.710 --> 00:17:19.610
and knowing where to go and
developing a whole network.

00:17:19.610 --> 00:17:22.510
I assume that was something
very intentional on your part.

00:17:24.750 --> 00:17:28.060
- Yeah, there were many
intentional choices with our crew,

00:17:28.060 --> 00:17:31.790
of specific times when we
needed somebody who's bilingual,

00:17:31.790 --> 00:17:34.400
who's Korean, who understood the culture.

00:17:34.400 --> 00:17:39.400
And Julia was one of those,
as our casting director.

00:17:39.435 --> 00:17:41.926
'Cause she would have to
be talking to parents.

00:17:41.926 --> 00:17:44.570
And she would understand the nuances

00:17:44.570 --> 00:17:46.020
of exactly what we're looking for.

00:17:46.020 --> 00:17:49.230
Like, which generation this child is,

00:17:49.230 --> 00:17:50.930
when did the parents come over.

00:17:50.930 --> 00:17:53.470
All these things have their own nuances.

00:17:53.470 --> 00:17:56.530
And then the other was
we definitely needed

00:17:56.530 --> 00:17:58.630
somebody who could speak
Korean as an editor.

00:17:58.630 --> 00:18:01.963
So Harry Yoon, who we brought on board,

00:18:02.858 --> 00:18:05.730
it was just so great to work with him.

00:18:05.730 --> 00:18:09.400
And I remember some producers came in,

00:18:09.400 --> 00:18:11.360
or some people from the studio,

00:18:11.360 --> 00:18:13.010
and we showed them a cut.

00:18:13.010 --> 00:18:15.630
And we totally forgot that
they don't understand Korean.

00:18:15.630 --> 00:18:18.900
And we didn't subtitle it. (laugh)

00:18:18.900 --> 00:18:20.240
And we're saying, oh yeah,

00:18:20.240 --> 00:18:21.750
this doesn't make sense for you guys.

00:18:21.750 --> 00:18:23.010
Because, you know, we were just able

00:18:23.010 --> 00:18:25.800
to edit it in Korean and English

00:18:25.800 --> 00:18:29.350
with that sort of shorthand between us.

00:18:29.350 --> 00:18:31.260
So there were key moments
where we just felt like

00:18:31.260 --> 00:18:33.900
we needed to cut to the
chase with that stuff,

00:18:33.900 --> 00:18:36.210
just because we didn't have a lotta time,

00:18:36.210 --> 00:18:40.650
and it would just help the
flow of making the film.

00:18:40.650 --> 00:18:45.030
- Well I am, again, pointing
out how wonderful it is

00:18:45.030 --> 00:18:46.810
to be working in this day and age

00:18:46.810 --> 00:18:49.100
where there are enough
people to choose from.

00:18:49.100 --> 00:18:51.070
When we were making "The Joy Luck Club,"

00:18:51.070 --> 00:18:54.240
we definitely could not find an Asian,

00:18:54.240 --> 00:18:56.580
Asian American casting director.

00:18:56.580 --> 00:18:59.180
We had a few other roles for Asians,

00:18:59.180 --> 00:19:00.810
but very, very, very few.

00:19:00.810 --> 00:19:02.410
They just didn't exist at the time.

00:19:02.410 --> 00:19:07.060
So, it's wonderful that we can crew up

00:19:07.060 --> 00:19:09.520
and cast up in the ways that we'd like.

00:19:09.520 --> 00:19:11.690
I had another question about

00:19:11.690 --> 00:19:13.700
why you were living in Korea at the time.

00:19:13.700 --> 00:19:15.280
And was it related to this film?

00:19:15.280 --> 00:19:18.230
Because this obviously took you back

00:19:18.230 --> 00:19:20.610
to some very deep roots.

00:19:20.610 --> 00:19:23.090
And how did your time in Korea

00:19:23.090 --> 00:19:25.343
affect the making of
the film, or vice versa?

00:19:28.362 --> 00:19:30.810
- So, I went to Korea to start teaching.

00:19:30.810 --> 00:19:33.550
I got a job offer to teach

00:19:33.550 --> 00:19:38.410
as I was writing "Minari" in 2018.

00:19:38.410 --> 00:19:39.920
And so it was a chance

00:19:39.920 --> 00:19:44.430
to finally have some kind
of stability financially,

00:19:44.430 --> 00:19:47.730
in terms of my own work.

00:19:47.730 --> 00:19:51.213
And my wife had carried the load for us.

00:19:53.460 --> 00:19:55.910
Especially because I had
asked for a couple years

00:19:55.910 --> 00:19:57.613
for me to try to write something.

00:19:58.860 --> 00:20:02.210
And so it was my time
to, you know, kind of,

00:20:02.210 --> 00:20:04.983
I gave it my two years,
let's go ahead and move.

00:20:06.120 --> 00:20:08.930
Going over there, what was incredible was,

00:20:08.930 --> 00:20:13.650
one, I got to live out my
own immigration experience

00:20:13.650 --> 00:20:17.280
of what that's like, to go
to a place I'd never lived.

00:20:17.280 --> 00:20:19.580
And my wife, you know,
doesn't speak Korean.

00:20:19.580 --> 00:20:21.203
She's Hong Kong Chinese.

00:20:23.470 --> 00:20:25.110
We were going there with our daughter.

00:20:25.110 --> 00:20:26.870
So we kinda were immigrants.

00:20:26.870 --> 00:20:27.703
It was very different

00:20:27.703 --> 00:20:29.560
from the way my parents
immigrated, of course.

00:20:29.560 --> 00:20:32.840
A lot more luxurious (chuckle) for us.

00:20:32.840 --> 00:20:35.790
But the other thing is we were placed in,

00:20:35.790 --> 00:20:39.410
where I was teaching was in
this place called Songdo.

00:20:39.410 --> 00:20:43.260
And it's in this area
with a lot of mud flats

00:20:43.260 --> 00:20:45.600
off the coast of Incheon.

00:20:45.600 --> 00:20:48.943
And that's on the western side.

00:20:50.280 --> 00:20:53.230
And what I found out from my mom was that

00:20:53.230 --> 00:20:55.300
that's actually where my grandmother,

00:20:55.300 --> 00:20:58.893
who I wrote the character
in "Minari" after,

00:20:59.970 --> 00:21:03.480
took my mom for about two
years after she became a widow.

00:21:03.480 --> 00:21:07.020
So, my grandfather died in
the Korean War as a soldier.

00:21:07.020 --> 00:21:09.660
And my grandmother took my mom there,

00:21:09.660 --> 00:21:13.510
and she would gather
shellfish in the mud flats.

00:21:13.510 --> 00:21:16.300
And that's how she
would, like, feed my mom.

00:21:16.300 --> 00:21:19.231
And my mom hates shellfish
to this day, by the way,

00:21:19.231 --> 00:21:21.000
(laugh) 'cause she ate so much of it.

00:21:21.000 --> 00:21:23.080
But I had no idea that she did that.

00:21:23.080 --> 00:21:25.070
And in my office, I could look out,

00:21:25.070 --> 00:21:28.250
and I could see people, women,

00:21:28.250 --> 00:21:31.050
mostly women going out and
gathering these shellfish.

00:21:31.050 --> 00:21:32.912
And it's a hard living.

00:21:32.912 --> 00:21:35.170
So it was strange for me to be there,

00:21:35.170 --> 00:21:37.070
to think like, oh my grandma did that

00:21:37.070 --> 00:21:39.880
so I can be here in this building,

00:21:39.880 --> 00:21:41.680
you know, in the air conditioning

00:21:41.680 --> 00:21:45.870
and doing something that's so much better

00:21:45.870 --> 00:21:47.470
than what she had to go through.

00:21:48.557 --> 00:21:51.010
So, I just felt a closeness to her.

00:21:51.010 --> 00:21:55.430
And that really gave me a type of compass

00:21:55.430 --> 00:21:57.083
as I was making this film.

00:21:57.970 --> 00:21:59.270
- Mm.

00:21:59.270 --> 00:22:01.520
Well, it's very apparent

00:22:01.520 --> 00:22:03.813
that some deep connections were made then.

00:22:04.849 --> 00:22:09.600
And having gone on that personal journey

00:22:09.600 --> 00:22:12.303
also is quite remarkable.

00:22:13.650 --> 00:22:16.580
The other thing you said earlier was

00:22:16.580 --> 00:22:17.580
being a little bit nervous

00:22:17.580 --> 00:22:21.400
about asking Steven to
speak with an accent.

00:22:21.400 --> 00:22:23.720
Do you feel like we've come a ways away,

00:22:23.720 --> 00:22:28.720
now that we have many more
projects and filmmakers

00:22:29.050 --> 00:22:32.220
and talent that are surfacing?

00:22:32.220 --> 00:22:36.260
Do you feel that it is now much easier

00:22:36.260 --> 00:22:39.080
to make a film like "Minari"

00:22:39.080 --> 00:22:41.150
and not be nervous about that?

00:22:41.150 --> 00:22:43.980
Because we've gotten over
the extreme sensitivity

00:22:43.980 --> 00:22:47.760
that existed when we had
so few opportunities?

00:22:47.760 --> 00:22:51.960
- Yeah, I mean, I kinda
go both ways on it.

00:22:51.960 --> 00:22:54.280
I do feel like there are reasons

00:22:54.280 --> 00:22:56.793
to be optimistic and hopeful.

00:22:57.670 --> 00:22:59.893
I still think it's a little hard.

00:23:00.890 --> 00:23:03.707
Because, you know, I think before us,

00:23:03.707 --> 00:23:08.707
"The Farewell" was being listed
often as one of the first

00:23:09.570 --> 00:23:14.270
to have so much Chinese
language dialogue in the film.

00:23:14.270 --> 00:23:16.570
And in many ways, they paved the way.

00:23:16.570 --> 00:23:19.260
We often used "The Farewell" as an example

00:23:19.260 --> 00:23:23.440
for why we can get away with
using a lotta Korean dialogue.

00:23:23.440 --> 00:23:27.190
Because, to be honest,
there were hard discussions

00:23:28.420 --> 00:23:30.190
in which there were
people who were worried

00:23:30.190 --> 00:23:33.173
about us using so much Korean in our film.

00:23:34.060 --> 00:23:37.200
And I think that it
also limited our budget.

00:23:37.200 --> 00:23:40.280
Like, the fact that we
were shooting this film

00:23:40.280 --> 00:23:41.630
for under 2 million.

00:23:41.630 --> 00:23:45.680
That was reflective of the type
of film that we were making,

00:23:45.680 --> 00:23:48.290
because it was not English language.

00:23:48.290 --> 00:23:51.620
And then you signed the
release of this film,

00:23:51.620 --> 00:23:53.720
just that level of confusion about,

00:23:53.720 --> 00:23:56.253
is this an American film,
is this a foreign film?

00:23:58.840 --> 00:24:02.563
So, my thought on it now is like,

00:24:03.420 --> 00:24:06.480
you know, what Lulu did was
incredible, with "The Farewell,"

00:24:06.480 --> 00:24:08.820
and that entire team.

00:24:08.820 --> 00:24:11.490
I hope that our team,
as the "Minari" team,

00:24:11.490 --> 00:24:14.670
that we also helped make
it easier for people

00:24:14.670 --> 00:24:15.960
in what they wanna do

00:24:15.960 --> 00:24:18.670
to tell the story that they wanna tell.

00:24:18.670 --> 00:24:20.033
To prove that, hey,

00:24:21.260 --> 00:24:24.620
there is an audience for stuff like this.

00:24:24.620 --> 00:24:29.620
And also that you can create
roles that are, you know,

00:24:29.950 --> 00:24:32.240
all different types of roles for actors.

00:24:32.240 --> 00:24:37.223
And that they can be very
complex and interesting.

00:24:38.090 --> 00:24:40.068
Yeah, I don't know if
that answers the question.

00:24:40.068 --> 00:24:42.100
- No, it does, it does.

00:24:42.100 --> 00:24:43.960
It's just interesting to
see how far we've come,

00:24:43.960 --> 00:24:44.870
and things are changing.

00:24:44.870 --> 00:24:47.610
There's still a lot of
anti-Asian hate crimes,

00:24:47.610 --> 00:24:50.930
and so there's, in my mind,
good news, bad news every day.

00:24:50.930 --> 00:24:53.020
Really good news (chuckle)
and really bad news.

00:24:53.020 --> 00:24:56.950
It's not a straight line whatsoever.

00:24:56.950 --> 00:24:58.470
But, you know, we do what we can.

00:24:58.470 --> 00:25:02.100
And this film really,
for many people, I think,

00:25:02.100 --> 00:25:04.460
was just another giant leap forward.

00:25:04.460 --> 00:25:07.665
I mean, let's face it, Koreans
are killing it these days.

00:25:07.665 --> 00:25:10.610
(laugh) Between "Squid Game,"

00:25:10.610 --> 00:25:14.100
and "Pachinko" is another beloved series.

00:25:14.100 --> 00:25:18.640
And so just, again, as
Bong Joon-ho famously said,

00:25:18.640 --> 00:25:20.060
that one inch of subtitles,

00:25:20.060 --> 00:25:21.380
if we can get over that obstacle,

00:25:21.380 --> 00:25:23.500
so much more is available to us.

00:25:23.500 --> 00:25:25.070
- [Lee Isaac] Yeah, absolutely.

00:25:25.070 --> 00:25:26.830
- And it means a lot, I think,

00:25:26.830 --> 00:25:29.320
for our children and our grandchildren.

00:25:29.320 --> 00:25:33.070
What does your child make of this movie?

00:25:33.070 --> 00:25:35.230
Have you watched it with
her, and also your wife?

00:25:35.230 --> 00:25:39.890
And did they learn a lot
about you and your personal-

00:25:39.890 --> 00:25:40.723
- Oh, there's a cat!

00:25:40.723 --> 00:25:42.820
- Excuse me for my
(Lee Isaac laughs)

00:25:42.820 --> 00:25:44.790
- Computer.
- I love that.

00:25:44.790 --> 00:25:47.430
- While you were making this.

00:25:47.430 --> 00:25:49.800
And how did it change

00:25:49.800 --> 00:25:51.850
conversations you had in your own family?

00:25:55.620 --> 00:25:58.700
- I do feel like it brought out,

00:25:58.700 --> 00:26:01.520
working on this brought
out a lot from, like,

00:26:01.520 --> 00:26:03.560
my wife knows me very, very well.

00:26:03.560 --> 00:26:06.163
We have very open
relationship with each other.

00:26:07.150 --> 00:26:10.360
And I think what it helped me with

00:26:10.360 --> 00:26:13.260
was that I remembered a lot more.

00:26:13.260 --> 00:26:16.040
And I was able to contextualize
things in my own mind

00:26:16.040 --> 00:26:17.623
about my childhood as well.

00:26:21.130 --> 00:26:24.750
For instance, like, I've
always kinda downplayed

00:26:24.750 --> 00:26:28.770
certain things in my childhood,
or just not mentioned it.

00:26:28.770 --> 00:26:33.060
But then I was able to say to her, like,

00:26:33.060 --> 00:26:35.180
you know, I think my childhood
(chuckle) was kinda tough.

00:26:35.180 --> 00:26:37.240
I had some tough times.

00:26:37.240 --> 00:26:40.300
Like, half our farm burned down.

00:26:40.300 --> 00:26:41.693
And we just kept going.

00:26:43.790 --> 00:26:47.120
It's stuff that now I can contextualize

00:26:47.120 --> 00:26:49.490
and think about my actual story

00:26:49.490 --> 00:26:50.840
of things that I've lived through.

00:26:50.840 --> 00:26:52.710
And I can kind of say to her,

00:26:52.710 --> 00:26:56.980
okay, I think I have these
anxieties because of this.

00:26:58.890 --> 00:27:02.985
So it was nice to work
on it in the sense that

00:27:02.985 --> 00:27:07.583
I learned more about
myself with certain things.

00:27:08.800 --> 00:27:09.633
For my daughter,

00:27:09.633 --> 00:27:12.940
she just kinda grew up
with me making this film.

00:27:12.940 --> 00:27:16.590
'Cause I was writing it when
she was, like, four or five.

00:27:16.590 --> 00:27:18.913
And she came to set.

00:27:20.240 --> 00:27:23.960
And she got to see the baby
chickens and all that stuff.

00:27:23.960 --> 00:27:27.320
And then she got to see this awards run.

00:27:27.320 --> 00:27:30.853
She seems immensely proud of
what's (chuckle) going on.

00:27:31.840 --> 00:27:36.840
And yeah, it's just been
fun to share this with her.

00:27:36.910 --> 00:27:39.810
To be able to do the work with my family

00:27:39.810 --> 00:27:41.030
and not in spite of them.

00:27:41.030 --> 00:27:43.310
Like, somehow to be together with them.

00:27:43.310 --> 00:27:44.450
Like, they came to Tulsa,

00:27:44.450 --> 00:27:46.990
and all the award stuff, I was at home.

00:27:46.990 --> 00:27:49.170
I was doing these Zoom calls
for all that award stuff.

00:27:49.170 --> 00:27:51.990
So I'd have an interview
with Hollywood Reporter,

00:27:51.990 --> 00:27:54.660
then I would come outta the
room and they're sitting there.

00:27:54.660 --> 00:27:57.650
And I'm, like, trying
to shake off my nerves

00:27:57.650 --> 00:27:58.800
and all (chuckle) that stuff with them.

00:27:58.800 --> 00:28:02.380
So we were together for all
of it, which was really great.

00:28:02.380 --> 00:28:03.480
- And so I was wondering if

00:28:03.480 --> 00:28:05.980
there's something you wanted
to share with this audience

00:28:05.980 --> 00:28:09.630
about how you deal with your emotions,

00:28:09.630 --> 00:28:14.553
and the mining of them for your work.

00:28:17.205 --> 00:28:18.038
- Yeah.

00:28:21.210 --> 00:28:22.610
There's a part in the film

00:28:22.610 --> 00:28:27.610
where Jacob's yelling
and he's having a fight.

00:28:28.040 --> 00:28:30.300
And he says, "I've been
at this for 10 years."

00:28:30.300 --> 00:28:32.420
And he, like, bangs the table.

00:28:32.420 --> 00:28:37.420
And that came out of me
writing at the coffee shop,

00:28:37.650 --> 00:28:38.560
where I was going every day.

00:28:38.560 --> 00:28:40.870
And I'm banging that into the dialogue.

00:28:40.870 --> 00:28:41.703
I've been at this for 10 years!

00:28:41.703 --> 00:28:44.263
You know, that kinda thing.

00:28:51.020 --> 00:28:51.853
Yeah, it's strange.

00:28:51.853 --> 00:28:56.490
When I was in Korea, I
sometimes tell this story.

00:28:56.490 --> 00:28:58.760
I thought this film was
not going to get made.

00:28:58.760 --> 00:29:01.120
Like, we got right to the finish line.

00:29:01.120 --> 00:29:05.390
And because of everybody's
schedules, I was told that,

00:29:06.270 --> 00:29:10.010
look, if we don't get this all
figured out in the next week,

00:29:10.010 --> 00:29:12.060
this film is not gonna happen.

00:29:12.060 --> 00:29:14.120
And if it doesn't happen
in this next week,

00:29:14.120 --> 00:29:17.180
then probably it'll never happen.

00:29:17.180 --> 00:29:19.600
Because, you know, this
actor has to do this,

00:29:19.600 --> 00:29:20.690
this actor has to do that.

00:29:20.690 --> 00:29:21.850
And then by then, it's like

00:29:21.850 --> 00:29:24.240
maybe five years from
now we'll make the film.

00:29:24.240 --> 00:29:25.690
That's kinda what I was told.

00:29:27.380 --> 00:29:29.883
That reality meant, okay,
this film is gonna die.

00:29:31.717 --> 00:29:34.940
And something weird happened,

00:29:34.940 --> 00:29:37.230
was that I just hung out

00:29:37.230 --> 00:29:39.570
with my wife and daughter that weekend.

00:29:39.570 --> 00:29:44.570
And I just felt deeply at peace

00:29:45.550 --> 00:29:47.560
and deeply grateful for them,

00:29:47.560 --> 00:29:50.100
in this way that it just washed over me.

00:29:50.100 --> 00:29:54.200
This feeling of thanksgiving and peace

00:29:55.668 --> 00:29:58.410
on a very spiritual level, I'd say.

00:29:58.410 --> 00:30:00.070
And like, I just felt like

00:30:00.070 --> 00:30:02.610
I had no feeling of regrets in life.

00:30:02.610 --> 00:30:04.530
I can move on now.

00:30:04.530 --> 00:30:07.550
I realized I came this close to it.

00:30:07.550 --> 00:30:10.343
It would've been nice, but
I have everything I need.

00:30:12.011 --> 00:30:14.730
And once I had that feeling, like,

00:30:14.730 --> 00:30:19.010
I just really said goodbye to filmmaking.

00:30:19.010 --> 00:30:22.003
And then the next week, it happened.

00:30:22.003 --> 00:30:23.870
We got the green light.

00:30:23.870 --> 00:30:25.523
Everything fell into place.

00:30:26.470 --> 00:30:28.970
It was late, but it just, like,

00:30:28.970 --> 00:30:32.290
I needed to go through
that experience somehow,

00:30:32.290 --> 00:30:34.820
on this cosmic level, I think.

00:30:34.820 --> 00:30:37.040
And I carried that into this film,

00:30:37.040 --> 00:30:39.540
'cause I felt like that's
what Jacob goes through.

00:30:40.800 --> 00:30:43.350
And I've been carrying
that with me since then,

00:30:43.350 --> 00:30:45.280
this feeling of an anchor,

00:30:45.280 --> 00:30:48.900
of what it is that kinda
grounds me in this work.

00:30:48.900 --> 00:30:51.820
And that's why I feel like

00:30:51.820 --> 00:30:54.723
I don't get too anxious nowadays with it.

00:30:58.573 --> 00:31:00.980
The truth is, like, the
fears are always there

00:31:00.980 --> 00:31:03.180
for us in this industry,
as you know, Janet.

00:31:04.120 --> 00:31:05.310
Even after success,

00:31:05.310 --> 00:31:06.623
what you start to hear is,

00:31:07.480 --> 00:31:09.530
you only have three years to make a film.

00:31:10.420 --> 00:31:13.530
You know, otherwise people are
gonna start forgetting you.

00:31:13.530 --> 00:31:16.460
I've been hearing messages
like that quite a lot.

00:31:16.460 --> 00:31:19.460
And I don't have anything

00:31:19.460 --> 00:31:22.170
that looks like it's
gonna go soon right now.

00:31:22.170 --> 00:31:24.320
And there are a lot of reasons why

00:31:24.320 --> 00:31:25.410
maybe I should be worried.

00:31:25.410 --> 00:31:29.670
But yeah, I don't know,

00:31:29.670 --> 00:31:31.330
that's kind of the anchor
that I've been having.

00:31:31.330 --> 00:31:33.890
And I hope that offers
something for people.

00:31:33.890 --> 00:31:35.480
- I'm sure it does,

00:31:35.480 --> 00:31:39.370
because what I often feel
and try to talk about

00:31:39.370 --> 00:31:41.050
is the spiritual challenges.

00:31:41.050 --> 00:31:42.600
Because on the one hand,

00:31:42.600 --> 00:31:44.710
what you did in that
moment with your family

00:31:44.710 --> 00:31:46.470
was to let go, right?

00:31:46.470 --> 00:31:48.360
Let go of something you were yearning for.

00:31:48.360 --> 00:31:51.190
It brings tears to my eyes
to talk about it actually.

00:31:51.190 --> 00:31:55.550
But, you know, you wanted so
desperately to do something.

00:31:55.550 --> 00:31:57.250
And in the end, you have to let go.

00:31:57.250 --> 00:31:59.530
Because you're not in control.

00:31:59.530 --> 00:32:03.673
And sometimes just by the
letting go, things happen.

00:32:04.830 --> 00:32:09.290
And yet, you have to maintain
a certain amount of faith

00:32:09.290 --> 00:32:12.070
and belief in yourself
(chuckle) to keep going.

00:32:12.070 --> 00:32:15.750
So it's really asking very, you know,

00:32:15.750 --> 00:32:19.340
the journey asks very
different things from you.

00:32:19.340 --> 00:32:20.900
Both to be thoroughly committed,

00:32:20.900 --> 00:32:24.630
and then be able to
detach at a certain point.

00:32:24.630 --> 00:32:28.590
So, it's a rather, you know,
it's like an accordion.

00:32:28.590 --> 00:32:32.170
You have to expand and contract
with the circumstances.

00:32:32.170 --> 00:32:33.730
But-
- Yeah, I think that's right.

00:32:33.730 --> 00:32:38.730
And I think that seeking
out success in this work,

00:32:39.520 --> 00:32:41.020
in film, in this industry,

00:32:41.020 --> 00:32:43.840
in order to solve some deep issue,

00:32:43.840 --> 00:32:46.410
I think that that's only
going to disappoint.

00:32:46.410 --> 00:32:48.779
Like, even if you find success.

00:32:48.779 --> 00:32:52.023
I honestly feel like
that's not gonna be it, so.

00:32:52.980 --> 00:32:56.070
- Well, certainly if one
is chasing fame or fortune

00:32:56.070 --> 00:32:58.150
or celebrated on that,

00:32:58.150 --> 00:33:01.550
there is zero guarantee of that.

00:33:01.550 --> 00:33:05.310
At most, we can achieve something

00:33:05.310 --> 00:33:07.190
that we wanna put out in the world,

00:33:07.190 --> 00:33:08.730
that we believe strongly in,

00:33:08.730 --> 00:33:12.313
and hopefully connects with
the audience, is how I see it.

00:33:13.600 --> 00:33:18.500
The external success factors
are simply beyond our control.

00:33:18.500 --> 00:33:19.333
- Yes, absolutely.

00:33:19.333 --> 00:33:20.650
- And it works both ways.

00:33:20.650 --> 00:33:22.410
Things that seemingly

00:33:22.410 --> 00:33:24.840
are going to be wildly
successful and are not,

00:33:24.840 --> 00:33:27.730
or seemingly, and as in
the case of "Minari,"

00:33:27.730 --> 00:33:31.350
very personal film that
broke a lot of rules,

00:33:31.350 --> 00:33:34.120
becomes a huge quote unquote success,

00:33:34.120 --> 00:33:35.940
in that it just touched people.

00:33:35.940 --> 00:33:37.750
And that gives me hope,

00:33:37.750 --> 00:33:42.750
is that sometimes the purity
of that reaction from others

00:33:43.070 --> 00:33:44.340
is what carries the day.

00:33:44.340 --> 00:33:47.498
And that's why many people just-

00:33:47.498 --> 00:33:48.520
- Thank you so much.
- Are still

00:33:48.520 --> 00:33:49.353
talking about "Minari."

00:33:49.353 --> 00:33:51.890
And as far as I'm concerned,

00:33:51.890 --> 00:33:53.250
you can take the rest of your life

00:33:53.250 --> 00:33:55.030
to make whatever you wanna make.

00:33:55.030 --> 00:33:56.400
There is no pressure.
(Lee Isaac laughs)

00:33:56.400 --> 00:33:58.420
- Thanks, Janet.
- Do not feel any pressure.

00:33:58.420 --> 00:34:01.470
It's not just three years.

00:34:01.470 --> 00:34:04.010
I believe some students have questions,

00:34:04.010 --> 00:34:07.000
so I'm going to take
some questions from them.

00:34:07.000 --> 00:34:07.910
Are there questions?

00:34:07.910 --> 00:34:10.450
If not, I have more questions.

00:34:10.450 --> 00:34:11.760
So if there are,

00:34:11.760 --> 00:34:13.963
oh, I see a lot of hands raised, okay.

00:34:14.870 --> 00:34:15.943
Justin Park.

00:34:16.980 --> 00:34:17.813
- Hello.

00:34:18.940 --> 00:34:21.720
My question is that

00:34:21.720 --> 00:34:24.100
it's very interesting to look at "Minari"

00:34:24.100 --> 00:34:27.223
in respect to the first
film that you've made.

00:34:28.309 --> 00:34:30.640
I'm not gonna attempt to say the title,

00:34:30.640 --> 00:34:32.623
but the one that was set in Rwanda,

00:34:33.710 --> 00:34:35.010
in the sense that

00:34:35.010 --> 00:34:38.870
that is where you are almost
kind of there as an outsider,

00:34:38.870 --> 00:34:40.540
kinda looking into their stories

00:34:40.540 --> 00:34:42.420
and telling their stories to the world.

00:34:42.420 --> 00:34:45.270
Where "Minari" is kind of
like almost the opposite,

00:34:45.270 --> 00:34:48.210
where the story coming from internally,

00:34:48.210 --> 00:34:49.963
from your own personal history.

00:34:51.270 --> 00:34:55.310
Which is why what you said
about noting down visual memory

00:34:55.310 --> 00:34:56.440
was so interesting to me,

00:34:56.440 --> 00:35:01.280
because, I'm guessing
even the Rwanda film,

00:35:01.280 --> 00:35:04.110
you may have not experienced
what they have experienced

00:35:04.110 --> 00:35:06.830
and what they lived through
in contemporary Rwanda,

00:35:06.830 --> 00:35:08.620
kinda the situation there.

00:35:08.620 --> 00:35:11.690
But the visual memory from
the film, I'm guessing,

00:35:11.690 --> 00:35:15.210
is still true to you during
your time you spent there.

00:35:15.210 --> 00:35:17.850
So, could you kinda discuss a little more

00:35:17.850 --> 00:35:21.580
about how you have navigated

00:35:21.580 --> 00:35:24.360
different stories that you've told,

00:35:24.360 --> 00:35:25.470
so far as filmmaker,

00:35:25.470 --> 00:35:29.650
and how you think you will continue

00:35:29.650 --> 00:35:32.760
to work on that navigation?

00:35:32.760 --> 00:35:34.700
Do you see it as it's kinda like

00:35:34.700 --> 00:35:37.550
an insider/outsider kinda type deal?

00:35:37.550 --> 00:35:40.990
Or is there more kind of a unifying factor

00:35:40.990 --> 00:35:42.973
that you have to your perspective?

00:35:44.040 --> 00:35:45.170
- Yeah, thanks Justin.

00:35:45.170 --> 00:35:49.333
Thank you for knowing about
that (chuckle) first film, even.

00:35:50.790 --> 00:35:54.000
That film was so fun to make.

00:35:54.000 --> 00:35:55.930
I went to Rwanda.

00:35:55.930 --> 00:35:59.373
I had about, like, a nine
page outline of the story.

00:36:02.470 --> 00:36:04.050
That outline was kind of based on

00:36:04.050 --> 00:36:07.910
something I had read as I
was researching the country.

00:36:07.910 --> 00:36:09.170
But when I got there,

00:36:09.170 --> 00:36:11.750
I just started to really work with people,

00:36:11.750 --> 00:36:12.840
and interview people.

00:36:12.840 --> 00:36:17.840
And almost like in a
communal sort of sense,

00:36:19.390 --> 00:36:21.180
we just shaped together the story.

00:36:21.180 --> 00:36:24.580
We started adding different
people's memories.

00:36:24.580 --> 00:36:27.040
So, there was that
similar element to this,

00:36:27.040 --> 00:36:29.330
where a couple of the actors,

00:36:29.330 --> 00:36:31.620
like, their actual stories,

00:36:31.620 --> 00:36:34.280
the two leads in that film,

00:36:34.280 --> 00:36:36.110
they would tell me things
that they've lived through,

00:36:36.110 --> 00:36:38.950
and then we would incorporate
them into the film.

00:36:38.950 --> 00:36:41.933
So we kinda did some of
that stuff with that film.

00:36:43.730 --> 00:36:48.730
But yeah, I felt very comfortable
in Rwanda as an outsider,

00:36:49.120 --> 00:36:53.440
in some sense, who felt like, oh,

00:36:53.440 --> 00:36:55.383
I felt very home there, as well.

00:36:57.160 --> 00:37:01.360
And I felt inspired by
the end of that project.

00:37:01.360 --> 00:37:02.990
I kind of told everyone, like,

00:37:02.990 --> 00:37:07.143
I need to go back to the US
and tell my own story somehow.

00:37:08.170 --> 00:37:10.550
I remember that experience inspiring me,

00:37:10.550 --> 00:37:12.510
because they kept on talking about

00:37:12.510 --> 00:37:13.963
we wanna tell our stories.

00:37:16.230 --> 00:37:19.090
You know, we just want more
chances to tell our stories.

00:37:19.090 --> 00:37:21.460
So I made a decision when I left

00:37:21.460 --> 00:37:24.470
that I'm going to try to help
them tell their own stories,

00:37:24.470 --> 00:37:27.880
and then I'm gonna try
to tell my own story.

00:37:27.880 --> 00:37:29.653
Like, there's some value in that.

00:37:30.810 --> 00:37:32.060
It took me many years

00:37:32.060 --> 00:37:35.770
to kind of come around
(chuckle) to doing that, though,

00:37:35.770 --> 00:37:36.973
in a weird way.

00:37:38.940 --> 00:37:43.940
But, as far as how I look at
what I'll do in the future,

00:37:45.800 --> 00:37:50.230
I'm just kind of interested
in doing everything, in a way.

00:37:50.230 --> 00:37:53.623
I'm not seeing any
limitation in that regard.

00:37:54.985 --> 00:37:56.440
I'm not, like, a systems guy,

00:37:56.440 --> 00:37:59.760
where I think systematically about things.

00:37:59.760 --> 00:38:01.170
I just kinda feel things out,

00:38:01.170 --> 00:38:03.960
and I'm just gonna feel out if
something feels right or not,

00:38:03.960 --> 00:38:05.380
and do it.

00:38:05.380 --> 00:38:09.830
And that's probably what
will drive me in the future.

00:38:09.830 --> 00:38:12.600
And then what I also notice
is that I do the thing,

00:38:12.600 --> 00:38:13.780
like, I make something,

00:38:13.780 --> 00:38:17.290
and then I get really good at
talking about it in hindsight.

00:38:17.290 --> 00:38:19.267
(chuckle) I don't know if
anyone else relates to that.

00:38:19.267 --> 00:38:20.100
And I kinda wonder

00:38:20.100 --> 00:38:21.860
if that's happened with
this film a little bit,

00:38:21.860 --> 00:38:25.720
'cause so much of this film
was intuition, to be honest.

00:38:25.720 --> 00:38:28.380
And then only later do I pick
up on some of the patterns

00:38:28.380 --> 00:38:30.930
and different things
that I was thinking about

00:38:30.930 --> 00:38:32.520
or influenced by.

00:38:32.520 --> 00:38:35.000
And then I'm able to talk about it.

00:38:35.000 --> 00:38:37.930
So, I don't like to go into something

00:38:37.930 --> 00:38:39.870
with a concept in mind first.

00:38:39.870 --> 00:38:42.660
I'd rather work heavily
on the intuitive side,

00:38:42.660 --> 00:38:46.110
and then be able to figure
out what is this thing

00:38:47.690 --> 00:38:49.323
afterwards in some way.

00:38:50.880 --> 00:38:51.713
- Thank you.

00:38:52.720 --> 00:38:54.700
- All right, Abraham Abatel?

00:38:54.700 --> 00:38:56.800
Forgive me if I pronounce your name wrong.

00:38:58.136 --> 00:39:00.336
- That was exactly
right, thank you so much.

00:39:02.520 --> 00:39:03.353
Hello.

00:39:04.450 --> 00:39:05.713
Hello, Lee Isaac.

00:39:07.520 --> 00:39:10.043
It's a pleasure to meet you.

00:39:11.160 --> 00:39:15.810
I guess my main question for you is

00:39:18.740 --> 00:39:22.450
when you were in the early
stages of your career,

00:39:22.450 --> 00:39:27.450
what was the one thing that
you wish someone had told you?

00:39:30.200 --> 00:39:31.273
- Thanks Abraham.

00:39:32.640 --> 00:39:36.873
Early stage of my career.

00:39:40.750 --> 00:39:41.993
I have a lotta these.

00:39:43.100 --> 00:39:47.130
Sometimes they're not as
helpful (chuckle) as others.

00:39:47.130 --> 00:39:49.610
I think you might be asking this though

00:39:49.610 --> 00:39:52.640
because you kinda wonder
with your own career,

00:39:52.640 --> 00:39:56.370
and you're kinda wondering what to do,

00:39:56.370 --> 00:39:59.400
and how your story might align with mine,

00:39:59.400 --> 00:40:01.410
and what you could piece from that.

00:40:01.410 --> 00:40:05.560
And it's just, my heart goes out to you.

00:40:05.560 --> 00:40:07.620
It's such a difficult process with this,

00:40:07.620 --> 00:40:11.420
with going into film, because
it's not an easy path.

00:40:11.420 --> 00:40:16.420
And it seems as though so
much of this happens to you.

00:40:16.700 --> 00:40:17.590
Like, you just happen to be

00:40:17.590 --> 00:40:19.730
in the right place at the right time.

00:40:19.730 --> 00:40:21.030
So, there's a lot of that.

00:40:22.540 --> 00:40:25.130
I would say the one thing,

00:40:25.130 --> 00:40:28.010
I think Janet alluded
to this so beautifully,

00:40:28.010 --> 00:40:30.470
is that there's a principle

00:40:30.470 --> 00:40:33.440
to why we're doing this thing that we do,

00:40:33.440 --> 00:40:35.780
that can often be a guide.

00:40:35.780 --> 00:40:38.350
Like, a belief in why we're doing it.

00:40:38.350 --> 00:40:43.350
And I think that is what
helps make this path more,

00:40:46.780 --> 00:40:47.890
it makes it easier,

00:40:47.890 --> 00:40:51.242
and it makes it less
fraught with disappointment

00:40:51.242 --> 00:40:55.030
if you're working with
certain principles of, like,

00:40:55.030 --> 00:40:57.540
wanting to connect with people,

00:40:57.540 --> 00:41:02.020
or do something good for
humanity, for society,

00:41:02.020 --> 00:41:02.853
things like that.

00:41:02.853 --> 00:41:04.560
If you're working with these principles

00:41:04.560 --> 00:41:06.490
and you're chipping away at it,

00:41:06.490 --> 00:41:09.470
I think that's always
going to be rewarding.

00:41:09.470 --> 00:41:12.573
And it's always going to be
the right thing to be doing.

00:41:13.780 --> 00:41:18.780
And it's the career type of
stuff that is really tricky.

00:41:19.420 --> 00:41:21.040
It can help you get lost a little bit

00:41:21.040 --> 00:41:23.440
when you put career success and all that

00:41:23.440 --> 00:41:26.910
before the stuff that might
actually get you there.

00:41:26.910 --> 00:41:29.460
And the stuff that might get
you there is actually, I feel,

00:41:29.460 --> 00:41:33.246
your inner life and your
principles, and things like that.

00:41:33.246 --> 00:41:35.900
I don't know if that answers
the question at all, but.

00:41:35.900 --> 00:41:37.590
- That's amazing, thank you.

00:41:37.590 --> 00:41:39.370
- [Lee Isaac] Yeah, thanks Abraham.

00:41:39.370 --> 00:41:40.483
- Byron Hamel?

00:41:44.550 --> 00:41:46.213
- Hi.
- Hey Byron.

00:41:47.830 --> 00:41:49.427
- Oh, there I am.

00:41:49.427 --> 00:41:50.413
(chuckle) Sorry.

00:41:51.390 --> 00:41:53.057
So first of all, I'm
really excited to meet you,

00:41:53.057 --> 00:41:55.830
and that's probably why I'm
pressing the wrong buttons.

00:41:55.830 --> 00:41:57.953
This is such a great film.

00:42:00.010 --> 00:42:01.920
I thought it was one of the best

00:42:01.920 --> 00:42:04.310
family dramas I've ever seen.

00:42:04.310 --> 00:42:08.890
But I wanted to ask about a
poetic thing that happens in it,

00:42:08.890 --> 00:42:13.890
when David first, when
he cuddles his grandma.

00:42:14.000 --> 00:42:19.000
And the next day, she's
starting to get sick,

00:42:19.247 --> 00:42:21.060
and he starts to heal.

00:42:21.060 --> 00:42:23.260
How much of that was intuition,

00:42:23.260 --> 00:42:24.640
and how much of that

00:42:24.640 --> 00:42:28.833
was something that you
designed poetically?

00:42:30.000 --> 00:42:31.313
- Yeah, thank you, Byron.

00:42:32.670 --> 00:42:36.470
Yeah, that was an idea
I had pretty early on,

00:42:36.470 --> 00:42:38.540
that there's going to be a switch off

00:42:38.540 --> 00:42:42.440
where we see David's journey.

00:42:42.440 --> 00:42:44.600
He's kinda going up in his health,

00:42:44.600 --> 00:42:47.410
and then Soonja, she's going down.

00:42:47.410 --> 00:42:48.430
So at one point,

00:42:48.430 --> 00:42:51.530
there's gonna be an
intersection for those two.

00:42:51.530 --> 00:42:55.550
So, I was very much
thinking about that moment,

00:42:55.550 --> 00:42:57.450
when it would happen.

00:42:57.450 --> 00:42:59.130
And I felt like it should happen

00:42:59.130 --> 00:43:01.610
at a moment that is very dark for David,

00:43:01.610 --> 00:43:05.360
when he's really thinking about death.

00:43:05.360 --> 00:43:06.193
I don't know why,

00:43:06.193 --> 00:43:09.210
but when I was a kid, I
thought about death a lot.

00:43:09.210 --> 00:43:10.820
(chuckle) It would stress my mom out,

00:43:10.820 --> 00:43:13.550
'cause she thought, there's
no way a kid your age

00:43:13.550 --> 00:43:15.960
should be talking about
death (chuckle) so much.

00:43:15.960 --> 00:43:17.543
I guess that meant, you know,

00:43:17.543 --> 00:43:19.643
I was gonna become an artist or something.

00:43:21.720 --> 00:43:24.120
So I just thought about that memory,

00:43:24.120 --> 00:43:25.150
'cause that was in my list.

00:43:25.150 --> 00:43:26.700
And I thought, I think this is where

00:43:26.700 --> 00:43:30.070
the intersection should happen with her.

00:43:30.070 --> 00:43:33.850
And that was the hardest scene to shoot.

00:43:33.850 --> 00:43:36.450
I never had a conversation
like that with my grandma.

00:43:36.450 --> 00:43:38.540
That never really happened.

00:43:38.540 --> 00:43:40.500
But there was something about filming that

00:43:40.500 --> 00:43:44.970
and watching Youn Yuh-jung
tapping David's shoulder.

00:43:44.970 --> 00:43:47.940
Every time I watched her
do that, I would lose it.

00:43:47.940 --> 00:43:49.630
And I just kept feeling like

00:43:49.630 --> 00:43:53.010
that was my grandma doing
that to me in that moment.

00:43:53.010 --> 00:43:55.440
And I couldn't get through it.

00:43:55.440 --> 00:43:59.930
And Locki, my DP, would,
like, hand me tissues,

00:43:59.930 --> 00:44:01.357
and he'd ask me, "Are you okay?

00:44:01.357 --> 00:44:03.570
"Do you want to move on?"

00:44:03.570 --> 00:44:04.580
Like, we do something else.

00:44:04.580 --> 00:44:07.300
And we had our schedule,

00:44:07.300 --> 00:44:08.900
we were shooting so quickly,

00:44:08.900 --> 00:44:10.630
I said, "No, we gotta do this."

00:44:10.630 --> 00:44:13.897
But the sound guys are like,
'We're hearing the sniffling.

00:44:13.897 --> 00:44:18.070
"Can you please calm down
a little bit?" (laugh)

00:44:18.070 --> 00:44:19.380
It was just a tough day.

00:44:19.380 --> 00:44:21.870
I can't remember any other scene

00:44:21.870 --> 00:44:25.350
that was so hard for me to
get through than that one.

00:44:25.350 --> 00:44:26.250
And even now I watch it,

00:44:26.250 --> 00:44:30.820
and I think I wish I could
try it a little bit more.

00:44:30.820 --> 00:44:32.640
'Cause I think I coulda made
that scene a little better,

00:44:32.640 --> 00:44:34.350
but I was so, you know,

00:44:34.350 --> 00:44:37.360
I was watching that scene
through blurry eyes.

00:44:37.360 --> 00:44:39.900
It was, yeah.

00:44:39.900 --> 00:44:41.965
- I don't think it could have been better.

00:44:41.965 --> 00:44:43.260
For what it's worth,

00:44:43.260 --> 00:44:45.160
I think it's one of the greatest things.

00:44:45.160 --> 00:44:46.250
- Oh, thank you so much.

00:44:46.250 --> 00:44:47.200
Thank you for that.

00:44:47.200 --> 00:44:49.820
I was not fishing for that,
Byron, but I'll take it.

00:44:49.820 --> 00:44:51.097
- No, but I did have one more.

00:44:51.097 --> 00:44:53.700
I know, I don't wanna
take up everyone's time,

00:44:53.700 --> 00:44:56.960
but did you give your real
grandmother your Mountain Dew?

00:44:56.960 --> 00:44:57.927
That's my last question.

00:44:57.927 --> 00:44:58.760
- I did not.

00:44:58.760 --> 00:44:59.593
No, I did not.

00:44:59.593 --> 00:45:00.650
For the record, I did not.

00:45:00.650 --> 00:45:05.290
And yeah, I hope that ends up
on a Wikipedia page someday.

00:45:05.290 --> 00:45:10.000
- (laugh) You can see people
clearly love this film,

00:45:10.000 --> 00:45:11.678
and studied it very closely.

00:45:11.678 --> 00:45:13.240
(Lee Isaac laughs)

00:45:13.240 --> 00:45:15.883
Alright, Adam Boncif next, please.

00:45:19.950 --> 00:45:20.783
- [Adam] Oh, hello.

00:45:20.783 --> 00:45:22.830
Hi, hi.
- Hey Adam.

00:45:22.830 --> 00:45:25.050
- So I'm actually not
Adam, I'm Alexis Brown.

00:45:25.050 --> 00:45:28.770
I'm using my work computer,
so I apologize for that.

00:45:28.770 --> 00:45:29.610
- [Lee Isaac] Oh, okay.

00:45:29.610 --> 00:45:31.730
- But I really, really admire your work,

00:45:31.730 --> 00:45:34.550
and I think it was an incredible movie.

00:45:34.550 --> 00:45:36.570
And I had a question about

00:45:36.570 --> 00:45:39.367
kinda like your process
and when you were writing.

00:45:39.367 --> 00:45:43.060
And you said that you wrote 80 memories.

00:45:43.060 --> 00:45:46.430
And there were, like, very bitter for you

00:45:46.430 --> 00:45:48.216
from your childhood.

00:45:48.216 --> 00:45:52.690
So with those memories and the
characters that you created,

00:45:52.690 --> 00:45:56.282
what was your process in
coming up with the timeline

00:45:56.282 --> 00:45:59.260
that kinda pieced everything together?

00:45:59.260 --> 00:46:02.230
- Yeah, thanks.

00:46:02.230 --> 00:46:05.303
I wrote those memories down on,

00:46:07.520 --> 00:46:09.480
so I wrote them on an Excel spreadsheet.

00:46:09.480 --> 00:46:11.480
I'm always like, why
do I tell people this?

00:46:11.480 --> 00:46:14.400
But I did it on an Excel spreadsheet,

00:46:14.400 --> 00:46:18.650
and I had them on different cells

00:46:18.650 --> 00:46:20.550
so that I could cut and paste them.

00:46:20.550 --> 00:46:22.090
And what I did was I took the memories,

00:46:22.090 --> 00:46:27.090
and I kind of separated them
into the different characters.

00:46:28.750 --> 00:46:30.550
And so once I had that, like,

00:46:30.550 --> 00:46:35.253
each character has a set of
memories associated with them.

00:46:36.420 --> 00:46:39.573
I would look at, is there
an arc that I could tell

00:46:39.573 --> 00:46:41.773
of each of these character stories.

00:46:42.860 --> 00:46:46.070
And not every memory ended up in the film.

00:46:46.070 --> 00:46:49.980
I definitely whittled it down quite a lot.

00:46:49.980 --> 00:46:51.700
But I definitely started with the memories

00:46:51.700 --> 00:46:53.610
and then moved directly into character.

00:46:53.610 --> 00:46:55.913
That's kinda where the
heart of it is for me.

00:46:57.370 --> 00:46:59.370
You know, I try to map
out their whole lives.

00:46:59.370 --> 00:47:03.030
I look at, if this film
is taking place in 1982,

00:47:03.030 --> 00:47:05.920
then what was happening
when they were born,

00:47:05.920 --> 00:47:06.990
what did they live through?

00:47:06.990 --> 00:47:08.140
I go through that.

00:47:08.140 --> 00:47:12.620
I have multiple tabs in my
(chuckle) Excel spreadsheet,

00:47:12.620 --> 00:47:17.610
where I kinda put out
their timelines, and yeah.

00:47:17.610 --> 00:47:19.540
So that's kinda where the process is.

00:47:19.540 --> 00:47:22.190
It's heavy on the character side.

00:47:22.190 --> 00:47:23.560
- Thank you so much.

00:47:23.560 --> 00:47:25.260
- Thank you.
- That's a beautiful answer.

00:47:25.260 --> 00:47:26.650
You're combining a little bit

00:47:26.650 --> 00:47:28.690
of your earlier science training, I guess,

00:47:28.690 --> 00:47:30.180
Excel sheets and tabs.

00:47:30.180 --> 00:47:32.140
Very organized and structured,

00:47:32.140 --> 00:47:35.109
but also very, very useful.

00:47:35.109 --> 00:47:36.250
Sungbin Moon.

00:47:36.250 --> 00:47:37.233
Question, please.

00:47:38.740 --> 00:47:39.740
- Hi.

00:47:39.740 --> 00:47:41.900
First of all, thank you for giving us

00:47:41.900 --> 00:47:45.363
great inspiration and stories
during the discussion.

00:47:46.760 --> 00:47:49.400
I'm an international student from Korea,

00:47:49.400 --> 00:47:51.470
and I've been living alone here

00:47:51.470 --> 00:47:54.010
in the United States for nine months,

00:47:54.010 --> 00:47:56.003
with this dream of film.

00:47:57.470 --> 00:47:59.240
Actually I saw this movie

00:47:59.240 --> 00:48:02.910
on my first flight from
Korea to the United States.

00:48:02.910 --> 00:48:06.180
So, it remind me of my family a lot.

00:48:06.180 --> 00:48:08.480
And what I wanna ask is

00:48:08.480 --> 00:48:13.480
how I can express my sincere
gratitude to my family.

00:48:13.790 --> 00:48:17.950
Because recently I'm only
immersed in my sadness,

00:48:17.950 --> 00:48:21.374
so I think I forgot how to
say thank you to my family.

00:48:21.374 --> 00:48:24.846
So, yeah, this is my question. (chuckle)

00:48:24.846 --> 00:48:29.846
- (chuckle) Thank you for that.

00:48:30.210 --> 00:48:31.483
That's so sweet.

00:48:33.560 --> 00:48:35.880
I mean, the fact that you're
asking that question, I think,

00:48:35.880 --> 00:48:40.230
is going to lead you to the
answer that's personal to you.

00:48:40.230 --> 00:48:43.900
And you know your family
much better than I do.

00:48:43.900 --> 00:48:48.900
So I trust that's something
you'll be doing all your life,

00:48:50.210 --> 00:48:52.763
trying to find how best to thank them.

00:48:54.650 --> 00:48:57.170
I'm trying to learn that
too, with my own family.

00:48:57.170 --> 00:49:00.060
How do I thank them,
how do I show gratitude.

00:49:00.060 --> 00:49:02.723
And it's not always easy.

00:49:04.070 --> 00:49:06.900
But I'm glad to hear,
like, what you're up to.

00:49:06.900 --> 00:49:10.220
I feel like a lot of people

00:49:10.220 --> 00:49:12.480
who have experienced displacement

00:49:12.480 --> 00:49:14.303
and have moved to other cultures,

00:49:16.340 --> 00:49:19.000
they make very interesting work.

00:49:19.000 --> 00:49:20.520
There are a lot of filmmakers like that.

00:49:20.520 --> 00:49:23.380
You know, Ang Lee was a
film student in New York,

00:49:23.380 --> 00:49:24.863
for instance, from Taiwan.

00:49:25.850 --> 00:49:29.223
And you could just see
his perspective on life.

00:49:31.470 --> 00:49:34.650
It's different, but it's very beautiful.

00:49:34.650 --> 00:49:37.770
And so there are many
examples of filmmakers

00:49:37.770 --> 00:49:39.870
who have just lived in different places,

00:49:39.870 --> 00:49:42.763
and who are able to tell a more,

00:49:43.670 --> 00:49:45.420
I would even dare say, like,

00:49:45.420 --> 00:49:47.520
a more universal story as a result.

00:49:47.520 --> 00:49:52.520
So, yeah, much courage to you.

00:49:53.100 --> 00:49:56.670
I hope your studies go well.

00:49:56.670 --> 00:49:57.557
- Oh, thank you.

00:49:57.557 --> 00:49:58.823
Thank you for answering.

00:49:59.660 --> 00:50:01.220
- Lovely answer.

00:50:01.220 --> 00:50:03.773
Emery Thanatitti, please.

00:50:07.500 --> 00:50:10.625
- Thank you for pronouncing
my name very accurately.

00:50:10.625 --> 00:50:12.470
(laughter)
- Yes.

00:50:12.470 --> 00:50:13.710
- Hi, I'm Emery.

00:50:13.710 --> 00:50:15.310
I'm also international,

00:50:15.310 --> 00:50:17.550
except I'm past the student part of it,

00:50:17.550 --> 00:50:21.840
and trying to make a
filmmaking career in America.

00:50:21.840 --> 00:50:24.710
And so, I'm suddenly nervous,

00:50:24.710 --> 00:50:27.030
but "Minari" is my favorite movie ever.

00:50:27.030 --> 00:50:30.150
I think when I first
watched it, I cried so much,

00:50:30.150 --> 00:50:33.090
because it really reminded
me of my grandmother

00:50:33.090 --> 00:50:37.770
when she brought the things
back from Korea to home.

00:50:37.770 --> 00:50:39.510
I'm from Thailand and Hong Kong,

00:50:39.510 --> 00:50:40.900
so my grandmother would bring stuff

00:50:40.900 --> 00:50:42.250
from Hong Kong back for us.

00:50:43.610 --> 00:50:46.620
And I think it really inspired me to,

00:50:46.620 --> 00:50:48.170
'cause this exactly the kinda movie

00:50:48.170 --> 00:50:50.160
I've been wanting to create my whole life,

00:50:50.160 --> 00:50:53.040
something that's about
family, about culture.

00:50:53.040 --> 00:50:54.280
And I just saw in one.

00:50:54.280 --> 00:50:58.040
So first I just really thank
you for letting me see that,

00:50:58.040 --> 00:51:00.470
like, this could totally be a reality.

00:51:00.470 --> 00:51:05.080
And I guess the question I
have for you is, as a director,

00:51:05.080 --> 00:51:07.200
one of the things that
everybody talks about in a movie

00:51:07.200 --> 00:51:09.440
is how well the pacing was.

00:51:09.440 --> 00:51:12.690
And for me, a lot of the
arguments were really real.

00:51:12.690 --> 00:51:14.840
I've experienced these growing up.

00:51:14.840 --> 00:51:17.440
And the pacing for what they say on screen

00:51:17.440 --> 00:51:18.750
and what they don't say,

00:51:18.750 --> 00:51:22.530
everything in emotions were
like, just done so well.

00:51:22.530 --> 00:51:24.280
How do you go about directing that?

00:51:25.485 --> 00:51:26.318
- Thanks.

00:51:26.318 --> 00:51:27.780
Yeah, first of all, thank you, Emery.

00:51:27.780 --> 00:51:30.143
That's incredibly touching.

00:51:34.271 --> 00:51:36.437
For the pacing of the film,

00:51:38.140 --> 00:51:40.700
there was a lot that
was internal with that

00:51:40.700 --> 00:51:42.253
as I was making the film.

00:51:43.820 --> 00:51:45.700
When I was editing, like,

00:51:45.700 --> 00:51:48.373
I would worry that I
didn't do it fast enough.

00:51:49.520 --> 00:51:52.490
It's funny, like on the day,
normally when I'm directing,

00:51:52.490 --> 00:51:56.030
I just follow my
intuition with that stuff.

00:51:56.030 --> 00:52:01.030
And then later I figure out
if I did it (chuckle) right,

00:52:01.220 --> 00:52:03.600
in the editing room, pacing wise.

00:52:03.600 --> 00:52:07.108
And so I would say like,

00:52:07.108 --> 00:52:10.870
I'm never the type of
director who has a stopwatch,

00:52:10.870 --> 00:52:13.890
or looks at like how long things took.

00:52:13.890 --> 00:52:16.180
Just whether or not it felt right.

00:52:16.180 --> 00:52:19.780
And we were going for

00:52:19.780 --> 00:52:21.880
something that felt
truthful with this one,

00:52:21.880 --> 00:52:25.600
more than, like, the energy
of the camera movement,

00:52:25.600 --> 00:52:26.690
or things like that,

00:52:26.690 --> 00:52:30.800
that are actually really
important in other types of films.

00:52:30.800 --> 00:52:35.437
But this one was a family
story, very simple.

00:52:36.690 --> 00:52:41.030
So I was just trying to keep
it feeling real, in a way.

00:52:41.030 --> 00:52:43.080
And then I worked with Harry Yoon.

00:52:43.080 --> 00:52:44.620
And when I was editing,

00:52:44.620 --> 00:52:47.986
he was really a compass for me.

00:52:47.986 --> 00:52:52.770
He kinda had down the
pacing that would work,

00:52:52.770 --> 00:52:54.440
the balance that would work for this,

00:52:54.440 --> 00:52:57.410
because there's something
that happens for me

00:52:57.410 --> 00:53:00.230
when I watch something way too much.

00:53:00.230 --> 00:53:02.760
Then I start to wonder,
like, if it's boring,

00:53:02.760 --> 00:53:05.270
if it's not working.

00:53:05.270 --> 00:53:07.740
And for some reason I was
watching "Stranger Things"

00:53:07.740 --> 00:53:10.600
and "The Mandalorian"
while we were editing.

00:53:10.600 --> 00:53:14.041
And I kept thinking, we
gotta go faster! (laugh)

00:53:14.041 --> 00:53:16.387
And luckily Harry was like, "No, no.

00:53:16.387 --> 00:53:17.940
"We got this, don't worry."

00:53:17.940 --> 00:53:20.880
And so Harry is like a brother to make,

00:53:20.880 --> 00:53:22.310
after that (chuckle) experience,

00:53:22.310 --> 00:53:27.310
'cause he kept me sane
and cool with what we had.

00:53:29.550 --> 00:53:32.413
He kept reassuring me, this
is good, this is working.

00:53:33.890 --> 00:53:35.570
In other words, intuition,

00:53:35.570 --> 00:53:39.043
but you also need some people
to help you, in my case.

00:53:40.700 --> 00:53:41.610
- Thank you so much.

00:53:41.610 --> 00:53:42.830
- [Lee Isaac] Yeah, thank you.

00:53:42.830 --> 00:53:44.250
- Okay, I see three more hands up,

00:53:44.250 --> 00:53:45.380
and I think we're gonna have to,

00:53:45.380 --> 00:53:47.100
if it's okay with you, Lee Isaac,

00:53:47.100 --> 00:53:48.650
we'll take these last three questions?

00:53:48.650 --> 00:53:49.770
- Oh yeah, yeah.
- Little bit later.

00:53:49.770 --> 00:53:52.660
Okay. All right.

00:53:52.660 --> 00:53:53.493
I'm gonna try.

00:53:53.493 --> 00:53:57.643
Nicolo Esquivelzeta Sohn. (chuckle)

00:53:58.770 --> 00:53:59.730
- Hi, yeah, Nicolo.

00:53:59.730 --> 00:54:00.940
Don't worry about the last name,

00:54:00.940 --> 00:54:02.823
it's such a difficult thing.

00:54:04.765 --> 00:54:06.320
What an incredible opportunity.

00:54:06.320 --> 00:54:08.080
Thank you so much.

00:54:08.080 --> 00:54:09.343
I'm so grateful for this.

00:54:10.450 --> 00:54:11.500
And I just wanna comment

00:54:11.500 --> 00:54:15.460
on how your film was just so universal,

00:54:15.460 --> 00:54:18.880
because I managed to connect with it.

00:54:18.880 --> 00:54:21.230
Even though I'm not an
immigrant from Korea,

00:54:21.230 --> 00:54:22.280
I am an immigrant,

00:54:22.280 --> 00:54:24.940
and I thought that immigrant experience,

00:54:24.940 --> 00:54:26.680
even though it was Korean,

00:54:26.680 --> 00:54:31.680
it managed to relate so much
to the general immigrant ethos,

00:54:32.050 --> 00:54:34.333
which I thought was fantastic.

00:54:35.930 --> 00:54:37.983
And, well, I just had one question.

00:54:38.888 --> 00:54:43.420
In an increasingly secular world,

00:54:43.420 --> 00:54:48.420
I noticed that "Minari" relies
on huge themes of faith,

00:54:49.660 --> 00:54:51.560
you know, and relying on faith.

00:54:51.560 --> 00:54:54.370
So my question is, in an
increasingly secular world,

00:54:54.370 --> 00:54:59.370
how do you manage to incorporate
faith into the narrative

00:55:00.010 --> 00:55:04.837
without becoming too
cheesy or disingenuous?

00:55:05.920 --> 00:55:07.143
- Oh, thank you, Nicolo.

00:55:09.920 --> 00:55:11.420
To answer your question, yeah,

00:55:16.820 --> 00:55:19.113
I would say I'm a Christian filmmaker.

00:55:20.930 --> 00:55:25.930
Christianity and faith is quite
a big part of my own life.

00:55:27.700 --> 00:55:29.100
And it was something that

00:55:29.100 --> 00:55:32.003
I would kind of shy away from sometimes,

00:55:33.360 --> 00:55:34.790
in terms of what I present,

00:55:34.790 --> 00:55:36.760
because it's such a loaded sort of topic,

00:55:36.760 --> 00:55:38.303
and it turns people off.

00:55:39.151 --> 00:55:44.151
My intention with it
is not to proselytize,

00:55:44.440 --> 00:55:45.930
or preach, or anything like that.

00:55:45.930 --> 00:55:48.610
But in this case,

00:55:48.610 --> 00:55:50.270
this story was so personal,

00:55:50.270 --> 00:55:53.500
it was so much about the
things that I think about

00:55:53.500 --> 00:55:55.260
and the way that I see things

00:55:55.260 --> 00:55:57.060
that I just wanted to be true to it.

00:55:58.030 --> 00:56:01.220
And that includes the very real doubts

00:56:01.220 --> 00:56:06.220
and the ways in which that I
think I make fun of my own self

00:56:08.670 --> 00:56:10.310
for the things that I believe.

00:56:10.310 --> 00:56:12.540
So, you hear that all in the film as well,

00:56:12.540 --> 00:56:16.110
those perspectives that everybody has.

00:56:16.110 --> 00:56:17.820
And I think on a spiritual level,

00:56:17.820 --> 00:56:21.610
everybody in that film kinda
represents some part of me,

00:56:21.610 --> 00:56:26.350
my own spiritual walk and journey
and perspective on things.

00:56:26.350 --> 00:56:28.260
In many ways, I'm Soonja,

00:56:28.260 --> 00:56:33.260
who's telling her daughter to
stop preaching this nonsense.

00:56:35.125 --> 00:56:38.724
I'm Jacob, who believes
using my own hands,

00:56:38.724 --> 00:56:41.950
and using my mind, and all those things.

00:56:41.950 --> 00:56:43.290
And then I'm also Monica,

00:56:43.290 --> 00:56:45.230
who just wants to pray for people

00:56:45.230 --> 00:56:50.230
and really believes and
hopes for better things.

00:56:51.690 --> 00:56:53.787
And even crazy Paul. (laugh)

00:56:53.787 --> 00:56:55.707
I have those sides of me too.

00:56:55.707 --> 00:56:57.680
So, I was just trying
to be honest with it,

00:56:57.680 --> 00:56:59.360
really exploring every character.

00:56:59.360 --> 00:57:00.230
And loving them.

00:57:00.230 --> 00:57:02.677
Loving that part of everybody,

00:57:02.677 --> 00:57:05.090
and really respecting and honoring them,

00:57:05.090 --> 00:57:06.723
and not criticizing any of them.

00:57:08.910 --> 00:57:10.883
- Awesome, thank you so much.

00:57:10.883 --> 00:57:12.074
- Thank you.
- All right,

00:57:12.074 --> 00:57:13.407
Patricio Aramos.

00:57:14.820 --> 00:57:16.240
- Hey guys, what's up?

00:57:16.240 --> 00:57:17.150
Hi, Lee Isaac.

00:57:17.150 --> 00:57:18.310
My name is Patricio.

00:57:18.310 --> 00:57:20.010
Thank you so much for your time.

00:57:20.010 --> 00:57:21.800
Thank you so much for this insight.

00:57:21.800 --> 00:57:23.260
It's been really, really dope.

00:57:23.260 --> 00:57:24.500
My question is

00:57:25.450 --> 00:57:29.310
what film do you recommend
everybody should watch?

00:57:29.310 --> 00:57:32.050
I'm currently looking
for something to watch.

00:57:32.050 --> 00:57:33.520
Other than "Minari," of course.

00:57:33.520 --> 00:57:34.951
Other than "Minari,"
(Lee Isaac laughs)

00:57:34.951 --> 00:57:37.500
what would you recommend everybody watch?

00:57:37.500 --> 00:57:40.240
- I'm gonna give a shout
out to someone who,

00:57:40.240 --> 00:57:43.820
I just spent a few days with this guy,

00:57:43.820 --> 00:57:46.447
and I gotta say, hands down,
he's one of my favorite people.

00:57:46.447 --> 00:57:48.350
And it is Darius Marder.

00:57:48.350 --> 00:57:51.037
And he made an incredible
film called "Sound of Metal."

00:57:51.037 --> 00:57:53.320
- "Sound of Metal,"
Darius Marder, perfect.

00:57:53.320 --> 00:57:56.020
- Yeah, go watch "Sound of
Metal" by Darius Marder.

00:57:56.020 --> 00:57:58.060
This guy is the real deal.

00:57:58.060 --> 00:57:59.423
- Okay, perfect.

00:58:01.020 --> 00:58:02.200
I Googled it right now.

00:58:02.200 --> 00:58:03.820
Oh, Riz Ahmed, nice.

00:58:03.820 --> 00:58:05.194
- Riz Ahmed.
- I thin it's on-

00:58:05.194 --> 00:58:06.170
- Riz Ahmed's an incredible guy, too.

00:58:06.170 --> 00:58:07.990
- [Janet] Incredible performance.

00:58:07.990 --> 00:58:11.280
- It's on Amazon, so
I'll be checking it out.

00:58:11.280 --> 00:58:13.130
- If you haven't seen it, please watch.

00:58:13.130 --> 00:58:14.681
- Perfect.
- All right.

00:58:14.681 --> 00:58:15.970
- Thanks so much.

00:58:15.970 --> 00:58:19.540
- Isaac Kau is next, and I
see Parvati just join us,

00:58:19.540 --> 00:58:21.770
so that's absolutely the final one then.

00:58:21.770 --> 00:58:23.400
No more questions please, after this.

00:58:23.400 --> 00:58:24.779
- We have to take a question from Isaac.

00:58:24.779 --> 00:58:26.423
- Yes, yes.
- Yeah.

00:58:27.753 --> 00:58:29.570
- Hi Lee Isaac.

00:58:29.570 --> 00:58:31.658
First of all,

00:58:31.658 --> 00:58:34.940
thank you for being a great
filmmaker with the name Isaac.

00:58:34.940 --> 00:58:38.680
Big inspiration to me.
(Lee Isaac laughs)

00:58:38.680 --> 00:58:41.350
Yeah, I'm a Taiwanese American filmmaker,

00:58:41.350 --> 00:58:44.810
so it's really cool that you
mentioned Ang Lee before.

00:58:44.810 --> 00:58:45.710
I'm also Christian,

00:58:45.710 --> 00:58:49.350
so it's really cool to hear
about that for your own story.

00:58:49.350 --> 00:58:51.883
My question is for your writing process.

00:58:53.750 --> 00:58:57.306
I've read up a little bit
on how you wrote "Minari,"

00:58:57.306 --> 00:59:00.343
and the whole diving into memories.

00:59:01.350 --> 00:59:03.650
And I think you've mentioned Dostoevsky

00:59:04.870 --> 00:59:06.900
as inspiration before,

00:59:06.900 --> 00:59:08.263
so I'm just wondering,

00:59:11.060 --> 00:59:14.430
yeah, just writing inspirations,

00:59:14.430 --> 00:59:17.540
who you would recommend reading,

00:59:17.540 --> 00:59:22.540
as well as your own maybe daily
or usual writing practice.

00:59:25.500 --> 00:59:29.063
And if you incorporate the
Bible into any of that.

00:59:31.730 --> 00:59:32.680
- Thank you, Isaac.

00:59:34.200 --> 00:59:36.900
Yeah, to be honest, I do
read the Bible every morning.

00:59:38.141 --> 00:59:40.260
I was talking to another
writer, Min Jin Lee,

00:59:40.260 --> 00:59:41.303
who does the same.

00:59:43.080 --> 00:59:45.750
And there's a lot of inspiration in there,

00:59:45.750 --> 00:59:47.030
especially the Old Testament,

00:59:47.030 --> 00:59:51.620
which is just filled with such
incredible and crazy stories

00:59:51.620 --> 00:59:55.743
that, yeah, you can see the
influence of it in so much.

00:59:57.020 --> 01:00:00.833
But I'm wondering if I
have this book around.

01:00:02.770 --> 01:00:04.837
One book I really liked
recently was called

01:00:04.837 --> 01:00:08.360
"A Swim in a Pond in the
Rain," by George Saunders.

01:00:08.360 --> 01:00:09.920
It's a really great book.

01:00:09.920 --> 01:00:12.700
And I love Russian authors.

01:00:12.700 --> 01:00:17.700
Like, Chekhov was a huge
inspiration for "Minari."

01:00:19.063 --> 01:00:20.540
He has this story called "The Steppe,"

01:00:20.540 --> 01:00:23.340
which is really wonderful.

01:00:23.340 --> 01:00:25.770
But he breaks down how to read a story,

01:00:25.770 --> 01:00:30.340
and how stories are told.

01:00:30.340 --> 01:00:33.673
I think that book is really
invaluable for writers.

01:00:35.208 --> 01:00:38.040
And then the question that you asked

01:00:38.040 --> 01:00:39.540
about disciplines and practices,

01:00:39.540 --> 01:00:42.210
I think that's really super important.

01:00:42.210 --> 01:00:45.900
Like, let's say you only
have an hour in the day

01:00:45.900 --> 01:00:47.440
that you can spare to writing.

01:00:47.440 --> 01:00:48.530
It's kinda like exercise,

01:00:48.530 --> 01:00:51.160
you only have an hour you can
go to the gym or whatever.

01:00:51.160 --> 01:00:53.550
I feel like it's really important

01:00:55.423 --> 01:01:00.400
to set that hour and be true to it.

01:01:00.400 --> 01:01:03.140
Try to be as consistent as possible,

01:01:03.140 --> 01:01:05.680
and do a little something

01:01:05.680 --> 01:01:09.490
that helps you get into the
writing, ritualistically,

01:01:09.490 --> 01:01:11.730
so that your body and
your mind knows that,

01:01:11.730 --> 01:01:13.573
okay, I'm entering my writing space.

01:01:14.753 --> 01:01:17.293
I'm normally not this
prescriptive about things.

01:01:18.390 --> 01:01:20.610
For many years, I was just the type

01:01:20.610 --> 01:01:23.050
who would wait 'til inspiration struck,

01:01:23.050 --> 01:01:26.820
and then I would sit down
and bang something out.

01:01:26.820 --> 01:01:30.050
And then, for many days,
I wouldn't do anything.

01:01:30.050 --> 01:01:32.760
That was never helpful for me.

01:01:32.760 --> 01:01:34.880
In retrospect, I realized.

01:01:34.880 --> 01:01:37.370
What ended up working is
just sitting down every day,

01:01:37.370 --> 01:01:38.893
even when nothing's flowing.

01:01:40.350 --> 01:01:42.680
Even if it means you sit down

01:01:42.680 --> 01:01:44.810
and you just drink a cup of coffee

01:01:44.810 --> 01:01:46.390
and you're not doing (chuckle) anything,

01:01:46.390 --> 01:01:49.250
and you're writing nonsense and gibberish.

01:01:49.250 --> 01:01:52.220
Your body just needs to kind of realize

01:01:52.220 --> 01:01:53.640
you're in that writing space,

01:01:53.640 --> 01:01:55.350
and your body starts to help

01:01:57.000 --> 01:01:59.203
get into that mode for you automatically.

01:02:00.270 --> 01:02:03.120
This was especially important
after I had my child,

01:02:03.120 --> 01:02:05.070
after my daughter came around,

01:02:05.070 --> 01:02:07.580
in which I only had a
certain amount of time,

01:02:07.580 --> 01:02:10.300
it was usually nap time
or something like that,

01:02:10.300 --> 01:02:12.450
in which I could sit
down and do something.

01:02:13.290 --> 01:02:16.770
And you'd be amazed by how
much that little discipline

01:02:16.770 --> 01:02:19.380
can actually help you
to focus and to work.

01:02:19.380 --> 01:02:21.610
So, I would highly suggest that.

01:02:21.610 --> 01:02:23.080
And I see on the side, yeah,

01:02:23.080 --> 01:02:25.060
it's "A Swim in a Pond in the Rain."

01:02:25.060 --> 01:02:27.250
That's the title of the book.

01:02:27.250 --> 01:02:29.185
So thanks for writing that.

01:02:29.185 --> 01:02:31.106
Thanks, Isaac.

01:02:31.106 --> 01:02:31.939
- Thank you.

01:02:31.939 --> 01:02:34.530
I'm gonna ask one more question
before I get to Parvati,

01:02:34.530 --> 01:02:39.010
because someone can not
turn her camera and mic on,

01:02:39.010 --> 01:02:40.573
and I thought it was an
interesting question.

01:02:40.573 --> 01:02:44.090
It's an African American
woman who says that

01:02:44.090 --> 01:02:46.410
she noticed that "Minari"

01:02:46.410 --> 01:02:49.900
resonated with so many
non-Asian people of color.

01:02:49.900 --> 01:02:53.040
And what do you think resonated

01:02:53.040 --> 01:02:56.640
particularly with African
American audiences,

01:02:56.640 --> 01:03:00.383
especially since she
has roots in the south.

01:03:04.350 --> 01:03:05.520
- When I was growing up,

01:03:05.520 --> 01:03:09.800
we didn't have an African
American community around me.

01:03:09.800 --> 01:03:12.903
It was quite a striking thing.

01:03:15.380 --> 01:03:19.740
It was weird, like, I remember
I felt such a deep connection

01:03:19.740 --> 01:03:24.273
to African American music and books.

01:03:27.160 --> 01:03:28.750
When I would discover these things,

01:03:28.750 --> 01:03:33.750
it gave me a sense of that
particular perspective

01:03:33.840 --> 01:03:38.223
of someone who is on the
outside in many ways.

01:03:39.100 --> 01:03:41.710
And I feel like we often relate.

01:03:41.710 --> 01:03:44.410
There's so many things
that we are similar,

01:03:44.410 --> 01:03:45.773
we have similarities.

01:03:47.260 --> 01:03:50.023
Very strong families, sense of family,

01:03:50.900 --> 01:03:52.920
that's multi-generational.

01:03:52.920 --> 01:03:54.703
There are these elements.

01:03:56.900 --> 01:04:00.603
The work of women being
so strong and vital.

01:04:01.710 --> 01:04:04.410
Yeah, there are just so many
touchstones that, to me,

01:04:04.410 --> 01:04:05.980
was always a deep connection,

01:04:05.980 --> 01:04:10.190
that I wonder if that's
a source of connection.

01:04:10.190 --> 01:04:11.063
I would hope.

01:04:12.760 --> 01:04:15.513
Yeah, but I really
appreciate that question.

01:04:16.470 --> 01:04:17.303
- Thank you.

01:04:17.303 --> 01:04:19.653
Okay, last but not least, Parvati Devi.

01:04:23.350 --> 01:04:26.140
- Hi, thank you so much for joining us.

01:04:26.140 --> 01:04:28.190
And also, I just wanna say that

01:04:28.190 --> 01:04:30.220
everything you've been speaking about

01:04:30.220 --> 01:04:32.610
with your experience with filmmaking

01:04:32.610 --> 01:04:33.570
has been really relatable,

01:04:33.570 --> 01:04:34.990
which is really refreshing to hear.

01:04:34.990 --> 01:04:37.120
- Oh, thanks, Parvati.
- Yeah.

01:04:37.120 --> 01:04:37.953
I wanted to ask,

01:04:37.953 --> 01:04:42.160
because "Minari" was such a
personal story for you to tell,

01:04:42.160 --> 01:04:44.560
I think it's really
easy for filmmakers to,

01:04:44.560 --> 01:04:46.030
like, when telling a personal story,

01:04:46.030 --> 01:04:47.040
they want full control

01:04:47.040 --> 01:04:49.430
over every single aspect
of the filmmaking process.

01:04:49.430 --> 01:04:52.590
So they want to have full
control over the editing,

01:04:52.590 --> 01:04:54.400
the way that the actors act.

01:04:54.400 --> 01:04:55.233
I wanted to ask,

01:04:55.233 --> 01:04:57.410
what is your mindset when it comes to

01:04:57.410 --> 01:05:01.610
trusting the different
people to do their thing

01:05:01.610 --> 01:05:03.260
and to fulfill their own roles,

01:05:03.260 --> 01:05:07.363
while also being satisfied
with your own filmmaking?

01:05:08.590 --> 01:05:09.423
- Hmm.

01:05:10.260 --> 01:05:14.650
There's definitely such
a big part of filmmaking

01:05:14.650 --> 01:05:16.840
that should, I feel, surrender

01:05:16.840 --> 01:05:21.780
and yield to the creative process.

01:05:21.780 --> 01:05:24.480
For me, that creative process is that,

01:05:24.480 --> 01:05:26.840
filmmaking is a collaborative art form.

01:05:26.840 --> 01:05:31.840
And sometimes filmmakers get into the trap

01:05:32.330 --> 01:05:35.800
of thinking that that is
a threat to our vision.

01:05:35.800 --> 01:05:38.900
But I view what we do

01:05:38.900 --> 01:05:41.810
as being an incredible opportunity,

01:05:41.810 --> 01:05:45.580
and it's something that
allows us to achieve something

01:05:45.580 --> 01:05:47.923
that is impossible on our own.

01:05:50.197 --> 01:05:55.197
So, case in point, when
we were making "Minari,"

01:05:58.600 --> 01:06:00.800
I had a stupid idea, I'll put it that way.

01:06:00.800 --> 01:06:01.730
That's the way I lead it off.

01:06:01.730 --> 01:06:05.310
I had some stupid idea
about the production design.

01:06:05.310 --> 01:06:08.880
And I got into a slight
argument with Yong Ok Lee,

01:06:08.880 --> 01:06:10.580
our production designer, about it.

01:06:11.693 --> 01:06:13.853
And then finally I threw down my card.

01:06:15.638 --> 01:06:16.820
I was like, "This is the
way it was in my family,

01:06:19.907 --> 01:06:22.350
"so let's just do it this way."

01:06:22.350 --> 01:06:25.238
And then Yong Ok said,
"This is not your family!"

01:06:25.238 --> 01:06:27.074
She, like, yelled at me. (laugh)

01:06:27.074 --> 01:06:29.480
Like, this is not your
family, this is the Yi family.

01:06:29.480 --> 01:06:30.830
And she was kinda saying like,

01:06:30.830 --> 01:06:35.820
this family belongs as much
to her as it does to me.

01:06:35.820 --> 01:06:37.830
And then she told me, you know,

01:06:37.830 --> 01:06:40.453
this is why this is personal to me.

01:06:42.026 --> 01:06:44.530
For me, that was kinda mind blowing.

01:06:44.530 --> 01:06:45.810
It put me in my place,

01:06:45.810 --> 01:06:49.750
where I realized everybody
has ownership in this project.

01:06:49.750 --> 01:06:52.050
Everybody is bringing
something personal to it.

01:06:52.050 --> 01:06:53.970
And because of that,

01:06:53.970 --> 01:06:56.370
it's growing into something
that's so much bigger

01:06:56.370 --> 01:06:59.930
than, you know, this small brain.

01:06:59.930 --> 01:07:01.953
It becomes something much bigger.

01:07:02.950 --> 01:07:05.380
And everybody was
bringing stuff like that.

01:07:05.380 --> 01:07:10.380
Like, our lighting guy was
a white man from Arkansas,

01:07:11.540 --> 01:07:13.490
who now lives in Oklahoma.

01:07:13.490 --> 01:07:18.490
And he's an incredible guy, Steve Mathis.

01:07:18.791 --> 01:07:21.237
He told me, you know,
"I took on this project

01:07:21.237 --> 01:07:24.297
"because I also lived in a
trailer home with my grandma.

01:07:24.297 --> 01:07:26.727
"My grandma kinda raised
me in the Ozarks."

01:07:28.706 --> 01:07:30.320
And with his lighting,

01:07:30.320 --> 01:07:31.890
there was something so deeply personal

01:07:31.890 --> 01:07:33.913
about how he lit that mobile home,

01:07:34.780 --> 01:07:39.680
in a very respectful,
dignified, humanizing way

01:07:40.550 --> 01:07:42.580
that could only come from him,

01:07:42.580 --> 01:07:45.340
in a way that, you know,
comes from his memories,

01:07:45.340 --> 01:07:48.786
all these different things
that are just so beautiful,

01:07:48.786 --> 01:07:50.490
So that's kinda the process

01:07:50.490 --> 01:07:52.290
that I'm still learning as I go,

01:07:52.290 --> 01:07:54.203
'cause inevitably I always think,

01:07:55.550 --> 01:07:57.440
when you're prepping
something, you get worried,

01:07:57.440 --> 01:07:59.120
so you wanna control it.

01:07:59.120 --> 01:07:59.953
And you figure out,

01:07:59.953 --> 01:08:01.910
this is exactly how we're
gonna execute the thing.

01:08:01.910 --> 01:08:04.190
But on the day when you're filming,

01:08:04.190 --> 01:08:07.500
it's those people around
you who really save the day

01:08:07.500 --> 01:08:09.130
and show you better ideas.

01:08:09.130 --> 01:08:11.630
So, that's part of the work
of being a director, I think,

01:08:11.630 --> 01:08:13.470
is to recognize that.

01:08:13.470 --> 01:08:16.060
And especially when you're
making a personal film,

01:08:16.060 --> 01:08:18.753
where you can sometimes be blinded

01:08:18.753 --> 01:08:22.060
by how personal it is to you
and how close it is to you.

01:08:22.060 --> 01:08:24.970
The other people help
get you out of your head.

01:08:24.970 --> 01:08:27.230
'Cause sometimes when
it's so close to you,

01:08:27.230 --> 01:08:28.840
it's hard to communicate those things.

01:08:28.840 --> 01:08:32.910
But other people will help
you bring those things out

01:08:32.910 --> 01:08:34.360
in the right way.

01:08:34.360 --> 01:08:35.720
Hope that answers the question.

01:08:35.720 --> 01:08:36.570
- Yes, thank you.

01:08:37.540 --> 01:08:38.970
That's something I will remember always.

01:08:38.970 --> 01:08:40.340
Like, it's as much your film

01:08:40.340 --> 01:08:42.310
as it is the other people
who are working on it.

01:08:42.310 --> 01:08:43.680
- Exactly, yeah.

01:08:43.680 --> 01:08:45.830
Yeah, you know, there's
nothing more rewarding

01:08:45.830 --> 01:08:49.120
than when you finish
something, finish a film,

01:08:49.120 --> 01:08:51.710
and everybody feels like
they own part of it.

01:08:51.710 --> 01:08:55.940
Like, yeah, that's just beautiful.

01:08:55.940 --> 01:08:58.550
That's exactly what you
want it to feel like.

01:08:58.550 --> 01:09:00.253
- That is.
- Thank you so much.

01:09:00.253 --> 01:09:04.500
- I think that's why we do
what we do, in some measure.

01:09:04.500 --> 01:09:07.050
You know, the process of
creating something together,

01:09:07.050 --> 01:09:10.260
and then having that result be shared

01:09:10.260 --> 01:09:13.370
with others that we may not even know,

01:09:13.370 --> 01:09:15.600
but somehow there's a connection made.

01:09:15.600 --> 01:09:16.470
Lee Isaac Chung,

01:09:16.470 --> 01:09:18.970
it has been such a
pleasure to talk to you.

01:09:18.970 --> 01:09:20.570
You really share yourself

01:09:20.570 --> 01:09:23.850
so genuinely and
generously and vulnerably.

01:09:23.850 --> 01:09:26.330
And I'm sure everyone listening now

01:09:26.330 --> 01:09:28.670
and listening later on YouTube

01:09:28.670 --> 01:09:33.040
will really embrace so
much of what you said.

01:09:33.040 --> 01:09:36.330
So, thank you, and I wanna
give a special thanks

01:09:36.330 --> 01:09:38.563
to your wife and child for comforting you

01:09:38.563 --> 01:09:40.130
(both laugh)
in that moment

01:09:40.130 --> 01:09:41.730
when you thought you might give up,

01:09:41.730 --> 01:09:44.020
and also to your parents and grandparents,

01:09:44.020 --> 01:09:46.410
and everyone else who's made a life

01:09:46.410 --> 01:09:48.970
that was so rich for you to mine

01:09:48.970 --> 01:09:51.650
in this most beautiful movie, "Minari."

01:09:51.650 --> 01:09:52.890
Thank you for participating

01:09:52.890 --> 01:09:55.413
in the Academy's Visiting Artists Series.

